{"id": "effect-7b8dd20b-9da9-5da4-a6b3-4c136cc32097", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346727069, "updatedAt": 1792346727098, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_findings.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_findings.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_findings.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_findings.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\supervisor_findings.md", "content": "# Astra inspection findings (implementation owner to reconcile)\n\n2026-10-18. This is review feedback within the existing assignment, not a new\nnumerical allowance. Preserve all prior attempts and accounting.\n\n## Harness inspection\n\n1. `harness.py` currently prepends candidate REPO/src to PYTHONPATH regardless of\n   --cwd. Clean-HEAD attribution must prove the actual imported anysolver origin\n   belongs to head_wt, not just change cwd. Add an explicit source-root argument\n   or equivalent exact import routing; record/verify import origin and effective\n   source/test hashes of the selected tree for each attribution run.\n2. Preflight TimeoutExpired or any Popen/parse/receipt exception currently reaches\n   finally without writing a receipt, updating budget or killing a timed-out\n   preflight child. Make accounting and process-tree cleanup unconditional for\n   every launched process, preserving partial stdout/stderr on every exception.\n   Do not rerun completed checks merely to recreate missing accounting; recover\n   existing timing evidence and label any uncertainty. No such exception has\n   been observed by Astra at this inspection.\n3. Receipt test counts should be parsed from JUnit or explicit named fields;\n   regex can omit failures if stdout contains '1 failed, 60 passed'. Verify\n   effective JIT/thread settings from successful runtime records, not env alone.\n   Hash every new diagnostic/test/oracle input used, not just the initial seven.\n\n## Analytical oracle — reserved scientific decision pending\n\nIndependent reviewer is assessing load equivalence. A free-width shell tied to\nan eccentric beam along one line is not automatically a uniform cylindrical\ncomposite section, even with correct net tip moment. Proposed explicitly scoped\nanalytical benchmark: exact cross-width plane-section constraints per shell row\n(shared ux,uz,ry; uy,rx,rz zero), with actual rotating eccentric beam coupling.\nThis is a defined verification model, not a claim that the free-width strip\nshares its exact solution. Independent derivation/review must establish validity\nbefore adoption. See supervisor_reference_review.md when available; Astra will\nrecord the adopted decision here. Constituent-resolved end forces and moments\nmust be derived, including their spatial/generalized conventions.\n\n## Astra scientific adoption — before any analytical FE assessment\n\nI independently checked and adopt the derivation and terminal loads in\nsupervisor_reference_review.md: positive My bends toward negative z;\nzbar=Ab*e/(As+Ab), B=E*(Is+Ib+As*Ab/(As+Ab)*e^2), k=Phi/L,\nRs=1/k-zbar, Rb=Rs+e, Ns=-E*As*zbar*k, Nb=-Ns; centroid moments\nE*Is*k and E*Ib*k about +y. Apply constituent forces along the final tangent,\nwith shell consistent tributary weights; do not add another composite moment.\nUse explicit exact plane-section row constraints (common ux,uz,ry; uy,rx,rz zero),\nclamp root only, and retain the actual eccentric rotating beam map. This scoped\nverification model has an independently justified reference. The free-width\nstrip remains a separate convergence study. Final-tangent dead loading is\nterminal-equivalent; intermediate states are not claimed to be circular arcs.\n\nBefore results: independent chord analysis predicts slow nonmatching refinement.\nRetain required nx8/16/32 evidence. If analytical errors decrease but the last\nrefinement criterion is unmet, additional nx64,128,256 are permitted in that\norder, only within the existing 3600s allowance and reserve. Apply the unchanged\n1% accuracy and 0.25% last-refinement thresholds to the final assessed pair;\nreport every level including any failed planned nx32 criterion. This prospective\nresolution extension changes no physics, loading or tolerance and makes no\nclaim that nx32 is adequate. Stop if errors do not decrease or resources do\nnot permit the next case; preserve the unmet gate. No external FE or retired\nprogramme execution is authorized.\n\nThe first worker checkpoint was inspected: identities match, runtime is Python\n3.13.9/numpy2.4.3/scipy1.16.3, baseline22+39 passed with both BLAS pools at1thread.\n\n## Independent inspection of first plastic result\n\nep-attrib-cr-plastic-6k returned stopped_at_limit (legacy converged=True),\nlambda=0.9423014322916665, minimum_load_increment_reached, 9 cutbacks.\nIndependent relative residual=3.2161147722756904e-9 at achieved lambda and\n0.05769856722306025 at requested lambda1; all three immutability checks true.\nIts 12 accepted snapshots agree with the reported last accepted residual.\nThis case supports partial-load interpretation, not a state/reporting defect.\nThe ep_attribution.py introduction/decision-rule prose still incorrectly says\nlambda<1 is a solver/reporting bug; correct that prose to the adopted decision.\nThe first combined CR batch timed out120.2373367s with no case output; preserve\nit and its full budget charge. Subsequent per-case output is retained separately.\n\n## Astra adoption of non-author source findings\n\nsupervisor_code_review.md R1–R5 are accepted as actionable source defects and\npart of the authorized connected route repair. The owner should implement the\nspecified focused regression cases, preserving baseline behavior outside the\nrotating-map path. All helper changes for R2 are inside nonlinear_static.py.\n\nR1: adopt fail-closed refusal for unsupported downstream dependencies on rotating\nslave translations; full nonlinear constraint graph composition is outside this\nslice. Inspect normalized compiler equations, not just coupling descriptors.\nKeep ordinary linear chains and upstream shell row ties supported.\n\nR4: exact rotating-map route requires work-conjugate internal force. Auto must\nselect it even with follower loads, retaining external load derivative terms.\nExplicit legacy rotated/consistent requests on this new route should be refused\nbefore constitutive activation with an informative ValueError, rather than\nsilently solving a different internal force law. Legacy models without an active\nrotating descriptor retain their current explicit-mode semantics. This is an\nintentional admission boundary for the new route, not a change to converged.\n\nR2/R3/R5: thread the existing map consistently through initial-field equilibration,\nmapped accepted-state commit/failure recovery, n_red==0 return, and finite restart\nDC row/load admission. Retain transaction, cancellation and caller-state guards.\nFor DC admission use full_u(q), not full_u(zeros), at the actual initial state.\n\nThe retained weighted rotation-vector map has a noncommuting 3D superposition\nlimitation. Preserve the adopted formula; document the actual demonstrated scope\n(planar finite rotations and tested states), not arbitrary nonuniform 3D objectivity.\n\n## EP diagnostic review\n\nAt initial inspection ep_edge_coupling.independent_residual fixes lam=1 and\ndc_summary calls it without res.load_factor. DC must reconstruct at its achieved\nunknown load factor and normalize consistently, not treat the proportional load\nvector as the actual external load. Include explicit force/wrench balance and\ndeep state immutability in final assertions, not just output dictionary fields.\nFull diagnostic output is evidence; acceptance requires automated assertions.\nOriginal-grid node identities are known from the fixture's construction; retain\nthem directly (or deterministic construction IDs), rather than coordinate search.\n\nClean-HEAD VK attribution verified independently: actual import origin in head_wt;\nelastic lambda0.5278676350911459/uz+0.004311600730850681 and plastic\nlambda0.46647135416666663/uz-0.016745015799163078 exactly match candidate.\nThe candidate source changes therefore did not introduce these VK observations.\nInitial clean-head launch failure (relative script path/import) is retained as\nep-attrib-vk-cleanhead; corrected evidence is ep-attrib-vk-cleanhead-2.\n\n## Correction and route decision after full admission review\n\nR2 is NOT an admitted defect: nonlinear_static.py already refuses corotational\ninitial stress/prestrain fields before reaching the linear helper. Preserve that\nguard and add/retain a refusal test; DO NOT broaden initial-field capability or\nthread an unreachable initializer merely to close this latent integration gap.\nThe code reviewer corrected supervisor_code_review.md accordingly. Active source\nrepairs are R1,R3,R4,R5; reference adoption and harness findings still apply.\n\nStop the serialized checkpoint detour. The public Slice3 restart contract can\nand should use initial_displacements + initial_element_states, with explicit\nequilibrate_initial_state=False, emit_restart_checkpoint=False. Use half6steps\nto-.10, resumed6steps to-.20, uninterrupted12steps. The initial lambda=0 is a\nNewton guess, not a material reset; for these shell-node dead loads its augmented\nunknown absorbs the previous lambda without a production API change. Preserve\nthe frozen Q4 checkpoint validation; do not loosen bindings or claim serialized\nrestart support. Document the observed pre-existing limitation separately.\n\nFor DC cancellation use progress_callback (accepted increment records), not\nstatus_callback, because DC does not emit the latter. Cancel after two accepted\nincrements; compare deep copies of initial displacement/state inputs and saved\naccepted snapshots; reusing those same inputs must reproduce the fresh resume.\nThe output diagnostics must reconstruct at result.load_factor, and use direct\nfixture node IDs. See reviewer addendum for detailed path reasoning.\n\n## Initial worker handoff reconstruction\n\nThe initial MaxTurns80 worker ended after a tool read with no final handoff\n(turn-bound completion, not service unavailability). Astra inspected evidence:\n18 charged attempts, used621.3494068000291s, remaining2978.650593199971s with\n900s still reserved. Initial seven owned source/test hashes are unchanged.\nUseful delivered artifacts are harness.py, ep_attribution.py, ep_edge_coupling.py,\nbaseline61 passes, completed seven-case attribution and pre-yield controls.\nNo new Slice3 test module or product repair exists yet. Numerous checkpoint\nprobes demonstrated the same pre-existing validation boundary; do not repeat.\nContinue the same Mistral owner from these artifacts with the adopted route and\nreview findings. This is partial implementation, not Slice3 acceptance.\n", "numLines": 162, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "162 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\supervisor_findings.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "effect-f5134b1d-2f47-519b-b932-4e808b25d14c", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346727775, "updatedAt": 1792346727800, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_code_review.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_code_review.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_code_review.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_code_review.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\supervisor_code_review.md", "content": "# Non-author source review — rotating MPC/work-conjugate route\n\n2026-10-18. Reviewed the five-file working diff against HEAD\n`b0202d335990a1fd1a647b7876ebde001aabe979` (+1226/-146 at inspection), the two\nnew Slice 1/2 test modules, and the necessary constraint compiler, initial-state,\nrestart and reaction callers. This was static source review: no imports, solves,\npytest, Git mutations or product/test edits. Existing 22+39 baseline passes are\nsupervisor-supplied evidence, not checks executed by this reviewer. Mistral owns\nimplementation and repairs. Line references below identify the inspected source\nbefore further worker changes.\n\n## Confirmed source defects\n\n### R1 — P1: downstream MPCs silently retain the linearized rotating-slave position\n\nEntry: `build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map`, corotational.py:1471–1511;\n`NonlinearRotatingMPCMap.apply_kinematics`, corotational.py:1312–1321;\n`jacobian`, corotational.py:1336–1369.\n\nThe ordinary compiler explicitly accepts and topologically resolves cascading\nMPC dependencies (assembly.py:201–270). The new map adds its nonlinear correction\nonly to each descriptor's three slave translations. It does not propagate that\ncorrection to downstream affine dependent DOFs. A permitted generic equation\n`u_Cz=u_Bz` downstream of a rotating slave B therefore becomes false in the\nreturned physical displacement: `u_Bz` gets the finite offset correction but\n`u_Cz` remains the original row of Tq+u0. A second rotating coupling using B as\nits translational master has the same missing upstream correction. Sorting by\nelement id does not repair this, because the implementation adds a correction to\nthe pre-expanded linear slave state rather than reconstructing its translation\nfrom the updated master translations.\n\n`to_linear_displacement` then removes the correction from B alone. Thus the\nexisting inverse-map linear constraint postcheck can pass while the original\ndownstream physical affine equation is violated. The map Jacobian and reduced\nforces may be internally consistent with this wrong manifold; their standalone\nfinite-difference checks do not establish composition correctness.\n\nMinimal discriminating test: attach an eccentric beam node B to shell node S\nwith e=(0,0,e), append another node C with a generic linear `uz_C=uz_B`, and\nset a compatible reduced shell rotation ry=alpha. At fixed shell uz=0,\n`uz_B=e*(cos(alpha)-1)` but `uz_C=0` after the current map. Check the physical\nequation directly; do not use only `constraint_residual_summary(to_linear(u))`.\nAlso cover a second rotating descriptor downstream of B.\n\nBounded compatible repair: full nonlinear graph composition is unnecessary for\nSlice 3. Fail closed before constitutive activation when any other admitted\naffine equation uses a rotating slave *translation* as a nonzero master. Use the\nactual normalized constraint equations/coefficients (respecting the compiler's\ncoefficient filtering), not only a check for another rotating descriptor. Every\ndownstream chain has such an immediate first edge, so checking direct use is\nsufficient to reject all unsupported descendants. Report the slave DOF and\noffending equation/element. Reject mixed translation-to-rotation dependencies\ntoo. This permits existing linear-only chains when no active rotating descriptor\nis present, and permits upstream shell row ties because those do not depend on a\nrotating beam slave translation. Rotating-slave rotations need not be banned\nsolely because they are downstream: their map remains linear. Tests should prove\nboth refusals and preservation of an ordinary linear chain/upstream shell tie.\n\n### R2 — P1: initial-field equilibration uses and commits the wrong manifold\n\nEntry: nonlinear_static.py:6098–6113 calls `_equilibrate_initial_fields` with\nT and the affine offset but no rotating map. Both helper signatures are at\n3454 and 3584. The candidate helper uses raw `T@q+u0` at 3508 and 3557,\n`-T.T@F_int` around 3522, and `T.T@K_T@T` around 3537. The commit wrapper\nrecords raw `T@q+u0` at 3626. The initialization-failure recovery similarly\nreturns the uncorrected affine state around 6122.\n\nFor residual/initial fields that require finite rotations, the initializer\ntherefore solves the linearized-offset constraint problem and accepts material\nhistory at that state. Subsequent main load iterations expand the same reduced\ncoordinates through the nonlinear map. The accepted displacement/history pair\nthen belongs to a different kinematic state, even if the initializer reported a\nsmall residual. This is a separate entry path from the correctly mapped main\nforce/DC Newton loops.\n\nRequired threading, all within owned nonlinear_static.py:\n\n- Add an optional rotating-map argument to both helper signatures and their\n  call-through. Preserve the existing path when it is None.\n- Expand both ordinary and backtracking candidates through the map.\n- Use `-map.reduce_force(u,F_int)` and\n  `map.reduce_tangent(u,K_T,F_int)` for the zero-external-load equilibrium.\n- Commit the mapped accepted displacement and return mapped initialization\n  failure recovery. Keep the existing single accepted transaction/finally\n  discard behavior, cancellation safe points and unchanged caller inputs.\n- Reconcile subsequent displacement/state activation with the accepted mapped\n  initializer output; do not reintroduce the pre-initialization displacement.\n\nMinimal discriminating test: a small coupled strip with an allowed elastic\ninitial bending/prestrain field that produces nonzero master rotation during\nzero-load equilibration. Independently check the returned physical offset and\n`J(u).T@F_int` using copied accepted state. Check constitutive state agrees with\nthat same displacement. Include failed/cancelled initialization to prove caller\nhistory remains intact and failure recovery lies on the correct manifold.\n\n### R3 — P2: fully constrained prescribed finite motion bypasses the rotating map\n\nEntry: nonlinear_static.py:5960–5971 selects `fully_constrained_displacement`.\nFor a non-checkpoint call it chooses `initial_affine_offset` or raw `u0`, while\nthe mapped state constructed earlier is used only in the checkpoint branch.\nThe raw vector then reaches constitutive evaluation/accepted-state recovery and\nthe returned result at 6006, 6026, 6058–6074.\n\nA model with every shell/master DOF prescribed and all beam DOFs eliminated by\ntheir coupling has n_red=0 and is admissible to the constraint compiler. Give\nthe shell a finite prescribed rigid rotation and matching rigid translations.\nIts eliminated beam must obey the finite rotated lever arm. The current early\nreturn instead returns the linearized lever arm from u0, despite active\ncorotational rotating coupling. This also risks pairing initially activated\nmapped state with uncorrected final recovery.\n\nMinimal test: prescribe a rigid rotation ry=alpha to all shell nodes with\ntranslations `(R-I)X`, constrain all remaining master DOFs, leave beam slaves\ndefined only by the coupling, then call the fully constrained public route.\nAssert finite beam offsets/rotations and zero strain for the rigid motion; do\nnot require an ordinary completed-load status where the existing contract uses\n`empty_reduced_system`. Cover direct and interpolated descriptors. Construct the\nfull constrained vector through the map before all evaluation/recovery/return\nuses, preserving the existing branch's status semantics.\n\n### R4 — P1: follower + auto silently changes the internal force law\n\nEntry: nonlinear_static.py:5344–5359 resolves auto to work_conjugate only under\n`and not follower_active`. With any follower pressure it falls through to\n`consistent`. In corotational.py:400–413 work_conjugate calls the B.T pullback,\nbut the legacy consistent path around 435–460 rotates the local force blocks\nwith E instead. These are different internal forces, not merely alternative\ntangent approximations. Follower external tangent assembly and rotating-map\nexternal reduction are otherwise present in the main force/DC loops.\n\nConsequently adding even an arbitrarily small follower pressure to a finite\ndead-loaded rotating-MPC model switches its internal mechanics away from the\nadvertised exact work-conjugate route. The baseline tests use dead nodal loads,\nso they do not exercise this discontinuity.\n\nPreferred supervisor-adopted repair: choose work_conjugate for rotating-MPC auto\neven when follower loads are present; retain the external follower tangent,\ngeneral matrix classification and net-imbalance MPC Hessian. Explicit legacy\n`rotated`/`consistent` requests need a conscious compatibility/refusal decision;\ndo not silently claim they use the exact work-conjugate force. Changing a tangent\nrequest must not accidentally change promised mechanics without declaration.\n\nMinimal tests: inspect effective mode under auto with nonzero follower pressure;\ncheck the complete derivative of reduced net internal-minus-external force at a\nfinite state against central differences, including external load derivatives\nand MPC geometric contribution. Add a limiting small-pressure comparison to\nensure internal-force dispatch does not jump as pressure tends to zero. This\nrequires a caller-level test as well as the existing map helper derivative test.\n\n### R5 — P2: displacement-control admission still uses the initial linear row\n\nEntry: nonlinear_static.py:3861–3864 checks `row_full@T` and rejects when it is\nzero. The actual Newton row later correctly uses `row_full@map.jacobian(u)`\naround 4088–4092. These can differ even at an admissible finite restart.\n\nExample: master uz is fixed, ry is free, e=(0,0,e), and control the beam slave\nuz. The linear MPC gives zero linear control row, while the nonlinear position\nis `uz_B=e*(cos(ry)-1)` and its finite-state derivative is `-e*sin(ry)`.\nAt a restart with ry nonzero the control coordinate is usable, but the current\npreflight rejects it before considering that Jacobian. At ry=0 the derivative\nreally is zero, so refusal there remains appropriate.\n\nThere is a second origin-state admission check immediately afterward:\n`initial_u=full_u(zeros(n_red))` at 3866 is used to reduce the proportional load\nand reject a zero result around 3892–3898. A force in the same nonlinear-only\nslave uz direction has zero reduced force at ry=0 but nonzero reduced force at\nthe finite restart. Fixing the control row alone would leave this false refusal.\n\nUse the actual initial mapped state `full_u(q)` for both control-Jacobian and\nproportional-load admission (including the supplied finite reduced initial\nstate); preserve the old linear admission when the map is absent. If this class\nof nonlinear-dependent controls is outside\nthe adopted scope, state that limitation explicitly rather than reporting it as\nfixed. Add a finite-state admission test and a zero-Jacobian refusal test.\n\n## Confirmed limitation requiring scoped claims, not an unapproved map redesign\n\nThe interpolated law averages additive rotation vectors, not rotations. It is\nnot equivariant under arbitrary superposed rigid rotations when master rotations\ndiffer. The existing rigid-motion test starts with all masters sharing one\nrotation, where this issue is invisible.\n\nAn exact algebraic counterexample: two equally weighted master orientations\nRx(+pi/2), Rx(-pi/2) have zero mean rotation vector and mean orientation I.\nSuperpose Q=Ry(pi/2). Principal logs of Q*Rx(+/-pi/2) have y components\n`2*pi/(3*sqrt(3))`; their x/z components cancel. Averaging gives\nRy(1.209199576...), whereas the superposed original mean is Ry(pi/2). Thus the\nbeam director and finite lever arm do not undergo the same Q. The common\nsingle-axis planar analytical family does not have this noncommuting-rotation\nobstruction.\n\nThe Path 1 average-vector law was explicitly adopted. This review does not\nauthorize replacing it with another SO(3) interpolation or changing public\ncompatibility. Restrict objectivity claims accordingly and retain this as an\nexplicit general-3D limitation/reserved architecture decision. A regression\ndocumenting refusal or admitted scope would be useful if a general objectivity\nclaim is intended.\n\n## Optimized pullback and state/reaction review\n\nNo separate confirmed algebraic defect was found in the inspected optimized\npullback. The precomputed translational projection and cross-product term match\n`D.T@f + G.T@(U.T@f)`; the shell analytic G follows the normalized tangent-frame\nderivative; two-node beam translation columns may be negatives by exact\ntranslation invariance. The shell rotation-column shortcut freezes a shell frame\nthat is independent of nodal rotations, as required. These statements are static\nalgebra checks, not new runtime evidence. Finite-difference G/Hessians and forced\nsymmetrization mean that the implementation's word “exact” denotes the intended\nderivative, not exact arithmetic or an independent tangent certification.\n\nThe main force/DC iterations, line-search promotion and reaction assemblies\nthread mapped full displacements and the appropriate net-imbalance Hessian.\nThe DC performance implementation delegates to the checkpoint-capable ordinary\nroute whenever a rotating map exists (nonlinear_performance.py:1520), avoiding\nan unmodified block fast path. Map apply/inverse helpers copy input arrays.\nI found no new mutation/cancellation defect in those inspected seams, but did\nnot execute cancellation/rejected-step/restart scenarios.\n\nRemaining acceptance coverage in the two reviewed tests:\n\n- Restart test at test_nonlinear_beam_shell_coupling.py:618 uses a direct\n  initial-displacement/material restart with dead elastic load. It does not\n  establish serialized checkpoint replay, DC split restart, plastic history,\n  cancelled/rejected-step atomicity or bit-exact nonlinear-map round trips.\n  The inverse helper test asserts allclose, while public checkpoint code uses\n  array_equal: floating round-trip representability remains a hypothesis to\n  test, not a confirmed defect from this review.\n- Wrench test around lines 569–615 checks each slave-to-average rotation torque\n  identity and total support *force*. It does not check total spatial moment\n  including deformed force arms, different interpolated-master rotation duals,\n  or nonzero prescribed support rotation. `_support_reaction_resultants` sums\n  additive rotation covectors; do not label those raw sums spatial moments in\n  a general finite-rotation configuration without the appropriate conversion.\n- Tangent tests validate matching, one-level direct/interpolated maps at selected\n  states. They do not cover R1 composition, R2 initial fields, R3 fully prescribed\n  state, R4 follower dispatch or R5 nonlinear-only control admission.\n- No full final 25-module regression or new Slice 3 gate result was available to\n  this static reviewer. Baseline passes do not close these entry-path findings.\n\nThe implementation owner should repair the bounded confirmed defects, preserve\nfailed evidence and unchanged acceptance tolerances, then return actual focused\ntest evidence for supervisor acceptance. No additional numerical authority is\nconferred by these suggested tests.\n\n## Follow-up correction and bounded DC restart recommendation\n\n### Correction: R2 is not reachable under current capability admission\n\nLater entry-path inspection found nonlinear_static.py:5683–5689 rejects any\ncorotational solve whose committed state contains initial stress/prestrain\nfields, with NotImplementedError. A rotating map exists only for corotational\nkinematics. Therefore R2 above is **withdrawn as an admitted-route defect**:\nthe raw helper is a latent integration gap if that future capability is adopted,\nnot an active Slice 3 repair requirement. The existing refusal should be retained\nand can be tested without implementing new initial-field mechanics. The earlier\nreport should be read as four confirmed admitted-route findings (R1/R3/R4/R5),\nnot five. This reviewer missed that preflight in the initial assessment.\n\n### Recommendation: use supported ordinary state restart for this DC gate\n\nThe task requires displacement/plastic-history restart and caller-input\nintegrity, not serialization. Stop the optional serialized-checkpoint probe\ndetour, preserve its failures as a separately identified pre-existing limitation,\nand use:\n\n1. Original dead shell-node reference load; target uz=-0.10, six DC steps.\n2. Same model/load/control coordinate; target uz=-0.20, six DC steps, supplying\n   copied `initial_displacements=half.displacements` and\n   `initial_element_states=half.element_states`, with\n   `equilibrate_initial_state=False` and `emit_restart_checkpoint=False`.\n3. Compare with target uz=-0.20 in twelve uninterrupted steps under unchanged\n   iteration/tolerance settings and existing restart tolerances.\n\nPrefer reuse of the original model/load for the resumed route; use a separate\nidentically configured model for the uninterrupted control. No field stripping,\nmanual constitutive-state alteration, tolerance relaxation or private load-factor\ninjection is needed. Ordinary returned corotational Q4 states can be unsealed:\nthe ordinary-restart branch around 2260–2274 explicitly admits their migration\nwhen displacement/history are supplied together and re-equilibration is false.\nDo not remove valid seals from caller data to bypass a validator.\n\nSource reasoning: `_prepare_initial_states` deep-copies material history\n(3012–3046); `_prepare_qualified_q4_states_for_nonlinear_solve` independently owns\nanother copy; DC q is recovered from inverse-mapped initial displacement\n(5873–5888); the DC helper copies q, computes initial_control from its mapped\nstate, and spaces subsequent targets between that initial value and the new\nabsolute target (3839–3915, 3960). Committed alpha/plastic strain is not reset.\nThe public helper call supplies initial_load_factor=0 unless restoring a\nserialized path (6444–6448). This zero is a Newton guess, not a plastic-state\nreset. It does reset step numbering/history metadata: the resume result has six\nnew records, not twelve automatically restored records.\n\nFor this specific EP gate all applied proportional forces act on shell nodes\n(diagnostics/ep_edge_coupling.py:119–122). Their rotating-slave force entries are\nzero, so map reduction of that load is independent of q and its MPC external\ngeometric tangent is zero. There is no follower pressure, moving tool or staged\nload. At the half equilibrium let reduced internal force be lambda_h*f. The\nfirst resumed augmented Newton equation started at lambda=0 is\n\n    K*dq - f*dlambda = -lambda_h*f + small_half_residual,\n    control_jac*dq = next_target-current.\n\nWriting dlambda=lambda_h+dLambda makes it the same first displacement equation\nas starting with lambda_h, up to the half solve's residual tolerance. The tangent\nis the same because the external follower and MPC geometric derivatives vanish\nfor these shell-node dead loads. Later iterations have the normal absolute\nlambda. Thus the default-zero guess is compatible with the same accepted DC\ntarget sequence and does not consume/reset material history. This argument does\nnot extend automatically to follower loads, load-dependent contact/tool paths,\nor loads on rotating beam slaves, whose external MPC Hessian depends on lambda.\n\nOne limitation must remain explicit: if an ordinary resumed solve rejects its\nfirst new step, its rolled-back displacement/history can be the valid half state\nwhile the returned lambda remains the helper's zero initial guess. Do not claim\nthat such a failed result has the half state's physical load factor. Check\ncaller-state integrity using the known half result; cancellation raises instead\nof returning a complete resumed result. This does not prevent the required\nsuccessful ordinary restart or input-integrity checks.\n\n### Diagnostic/helper conflicts to fix before another bounded run\n\n- `dc_solve` hardcodes `emit_restart_checkpoint=True` at diagnostic line 190.\n  Remove that requirement for full/split/cancel stages; passing the opposite\n  keyword through `**extra` would duplicate the keyword rather than override it.\n- `stage_dc_split` currently consumes checkpoint bytes and compares complete\n  step dictionaries/bytes (229–249). Replace with ordinary displacement/history\n  inputs. Compare half records followed by resume records against the twelve\n  uninterrupted physical records. Account for local resumed indices 1–6\n  prospectively in the comparison only; do not falsify the returned records.\n- `independent_residual` (139–154) hardcodes lambda=1. DC solves for lambda,\n  generally different from one. Evaluate residual at the actual result or\n  snapshot lambda, with the same reference load and copied state. Its reference\n  scale should also reflect the actual applied load convention, rather than\n  silently reporting a unit-load residual as a DC equilibrium test.\n- `stage_dc_cancel` installs `status_callback`, but the DC helper does not call\n  that force-control-only observer. Use `progress_callback` filtered to\n  event_type `nonlinear_static_step` / stage `nonlinear_static.displacement`.\n  Cancel the token after two accepted new steps; the next DC cancellation safe\n  point raises SolveCancelled. Merely raising inside the progress observer is\n  unreliable because progress callback exceptions are normally suppressed.\n- `dc_summary` hardcodes target_error relative to -0.20. Parameterize the target\n  when reporting the half solve as well.\n\n### Necessary evidence under the existing execution allowance\n\n- Both routes: half/full/resume status is completed; controlled targets agree;\n  positive plastic strain; final u/lambda and all physical plastic histories\n  agree within the unchanged existing restart tolerances.\n- Stitch half+resume accepted control/load/alpha histories and compare with the\n  twelve uninterrupted steps. The half solve's achieved -0.10 can differ from its\n  exact target by its stated constraint tolerance; report that error rather than\n  silently resetting the displacement to an exact target.\n- Retain deep before/after copies of supplied displacement, every nested material\n  history array/metadata, and half accepted snapshots. Prove unchanged after a\n  successful resume, a deliberately rejected resume and token cancellation;\n  then reuse the same untouched input for a clean successful resume.\n- Verify accepted snapshots have their own stable displacement/history contents.\n  If cancellation throws before exposing the cancelled run's local snapshots,\n  a focused test can capture `_increment_snapshot` outputs without changing\n  mechanics, then compare their deep-frozen copies after later trials/cancellation.\n  At minimum preserve/check the exposed half-result snapshots after all reuse.\n- Independently reconstruct the final/snapshot reduced residual at its own lambda\n  and retain actual constraints/reactions. Do not infer restart success from\n  unchanged input data or merely from the public converged property.\n\nNo numerical execution or product/diagnostic edit was performed for this follow-up.\n", "numLines": 366, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "366 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\supervisor_code_review.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "effect-150dfebb-70d7-5e49-83c1-e34512a0912c", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346728816, "updatedAt": 1792346728842, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_reference_review.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_reference_review.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_reference_review.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Github/ANYsolver/.development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/supervisor_reference_review.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\supervisor_reference_review.md", "content": "# Independent analytical review — Slice 3\n\nState: scientific review of the proposed reference, 2026-10-18. No product or\ntest edits, imports, FE solves or pytest runs were performed. The calculations\nbelow use section geometry and elementary rod virtual work independently of\nproduction assembly and MPC reduction. Supervisor adoption remains required.\n\n## Decision\n\nThe circular arc is a valid reference for an explicitly **cylindrical,\nplane-section strip benchmark**, including eccentric cases. It is not an exact\nreference for the free-width shell with one attached beam line solely because\nthe applied end resultants equal a composite pure moment.\n\nAdopt the supervisor's proposed benchmark constraints prospectively: at every\nshell longitudinal station all nodes share ux, uz and ry; set uy, rx and rz to\nzero. Leave the common ux, uz and ry independent between stations; clamp only\nthe root station. Retain the real finite-rotation eccentric beam MPC and, for\nnonmatching meshes, its actual interpolation. With nu=0 these width constraints\nsupply only the missing width redistribution, without Poisson restraint changing\nthe intended axial/bending law. Their reactions have zero work on the admitted\nrow motion. This defines a constrained strip benchmark, not a claim that the\noriginal free-width stiffened plate has an exact circular-arc solution.\n\nThe final-state dead load prescription below is sufficient. A follower-force\nAPI is unnecessary if only the terminal state is compared. The load ramp does\nnot then follow the circular-arc family and must not be reported as doing so.\n\n## Convention and section assumptions\n\nReference shell midsurface: (s,y,0), 0<=s<=L. Beam centroid: (s,y_b,e).\nPositive rotation phi about global +y bends the cantilever toward negative z.\nThe 0.01 x 0.002 m beam rectangle means 0.01 m in y and 0.002 m in z;\nchanging that orientation changes the oracle. The constituents are additive\nenergetic shell/beam components, including at e=0; geometric overlap does not\nsubtract material in this idealized model. Use linear elasticity, not the S355\nplastic curve used in the separate continuation gate.\n\nLet\n\n    As = W*t;                  Is = W*t^3/12\n    Ab = b*h;                  Ib = b*h^3/12\n    zbar = Ab*e/(As+Ab)\n    B = E*[Is+Ib+As*zbar^2+Ab*(e-zbar)^2]\n      = E*[Is+Ib+As*Ab/(As+Ab)*e^2].\n\nB is the composite bending rigidity about the neutral axis. For different\nmoduli use EA-weighted zbar and EI terms, but that generalization is not needed\nfor this assignment.\n\nFor E=210e9 Pa, W=0.1 m, t=0.001 m, b=0.01 m, h=0.002 m:\n\n    As=1e-4 m^2; Ab=2e-5 m^2\n    Is=8.333333333333e-12 m^4; Ib=6.666666666667e-12 m^4\n    E*Is=1.75 N m^2; E*Ib=1.40 N m^2\n    e=0:       zbar=0;                 B=3.15 N m^2\n    e=0.002:   zbar=0.0003333333333 m;  B=17.15 N m^2.\n\nThe initial x-axis beam frame in elements.py::_beam_rotation_matrix has local\ny=global y and local z=global z when no exceptional orientation is supplied.\nThus the required bending inertia is Iy=Ib above.\n\n## Independent kinematics and constitutive derivation\n\nUse curvature per reference longitudinal length k=Phi/L and phi(s)=k*s.\nLet t=(cos(phi),0,-sin(phi)), n=(sin(phi),0,cos(phi)). The cylindrical\nKirchhoff kinematics are\n\n    r(s,y,z) = r_s(s) + y*ey + z*n(s)\n    r_s'(s) = lambda_s*t(s)\n    lambda(z) = lambda_s + z*k\n    epsilon(z) = lambda_s-1 + z*k.\n\nZero total section axial force gives lambda_s=1-zbar*k, hence\n\n    epsilon(z)=k*(z-zbar),\n    Ns=E*As*(-zbar*k),\n    Nb=E*Ab*(e-zbar)*k=-Ns.\n\nAn equivalent independent derivation minimizes, over lambda_s,\n\n    U/L = E/2*[As*(lambda_s-1)^2\n                    +Ab*(lambda_s-1+e*k)^2+(Is+Ib)*k^2].\n\nIt gives U/L=B*k^2/2 and M=B*k. This is the extensible rod law with\nengineering axial strain, reference-length curvature and linear section\nconstitutive response. Small material strains coexist with arbitrarily large\nrigid rotations. Shell and beam centroid axial strains are generally nonzero;\nsetting both to zero is incompatible with e*k != 0 and the rotating offset.\n\nThe neutral-axis radius is 1/k, shell midsurface radius Rs=1/k-zbar and beam\ncentroid radius Rb=1/k+e-zbar. All share the same angle, not the same arc length.\nFor either centroid with initial z=zj, its exact tip displacement is\n\n    ux_j = [1/k+zj-zbar]*sin(Phi)-L\n    uz_j = [1/k+zj-zbar]*[cos(Phi)-1]\n    uy_j = 0; theta_j = (0,Phi,0).\n\nDo not compare the beam probe to the shell/neutral-axis radius. At e=0.002,\nPhi=60deg the beam and shell uz differ by exactly -0.001 m.\n\n## Constituent end loads and spatial moments\n\nAt the final tip use\n\n    F_shell = Ns*t(Phi)\n    F_beam  = Nb*t(Phi)\n    m_shell = (0,E*Is*k,0)\n    m_beam  = (0,E*Ib*k,0).\n\nShell force and moment are the width-integrated values. A consistent uniform\nedge traction uses tributary weights [1/4,1/2,1/4] for ny=2; their sums must be\none. Under the adopted row constraints any distribution with the same admitted\nvirtual work is equivalent, but uniform consistent loads retain a clearer\nphysical interpretation. Beam values act at its centroid tip node. Do not add\nanother total M=B*k moment: that would count the eccentric force couple twice.\n\nThe total force is zero. The deformed separation is e*n(Phi), and n cross t=ey,\nso the total tip moment about the shell centroid is\n\n    M_y = E*(Is+Ib)*k + e*Nb = B*k.\n\nThe equality uses Ns+Nb=0; the same total moment holds about the neutral axis.\nThis also checks the eccentric load signs independently. With e>0 and k>0 the\nbeam centroid force is tensile and the shell centroid force is compressive.\n\nDead global nodal forces equal to these vectors have the same terminal virtual\nwork as follower tangential forces. At intermediate load factor lambda, the\ncurrent rotation usually differs from Phi; the dead forces are then not\ntangential and their eccentric spatial couple differs from lambda*e*Nb. That\nchanges the loading path but not the specified terminal equilibrium. Convergence\nand residual evidence are still required, and intermediate arc comparisons are\nnot valid under this dead prescription.\n\nFor an additive rotation-vector covector p and spatial moment m,\n\n    delta_psi = J_l(theta)*delta_theta,\n    p = J_l(theta)^T*m,\n    m = J_l(theta)^(-T)*p.\n\nThis agrees with corotational.py::spatial_moment_from_rotation_covector (line\n739 at inspection). For theta=(0,phi,0), J_l(theta)*ey=ey, so a moment only in y\nhas p_ry=My exactly at 30 or 60 degrees. This shortcut is invalid for general\nmultiaxis rotations/moments. Total reactions must use deformed force arms and\nspatial moments, not raw rotation-vector covectors in a general state.\n\n## Why the free-width eccentric shell is not exact\n\nFor the proposed uniform cylindrical field, Ns is a nonzero constant when e is\nnonzero. Since t'=-k*n, shell membrane equilibrium needs an interface normal\nload proportional to Ns*k distributed across its full width. The beam's\nequal/opposite required normal load arrives through one coupling line. A shell\nwith y-independent curvature and no transverse bending/shear cannot redistribute\nthat line load into the required uniform normal load. End loads alone do not\nremove this local equilibrium mismatch. Width bending/warping or interface\nreaction redistribution must occur. The plane-section row constraints supply\nthe latter and make the integrated composite rod reduction legitimate.\n\nAn especially poor alternative is applying all composite moment to one shell\nedge or to the beam tip in a free-width model: resultant equivalence does not\nprevent torsion, shear lag or a local end boundary layer. Saint-Venant arguments\nalone do not establish a 1% bound for this thin strip and a tip quantity.\n\nThe concentric e=0 case has Ns=Nb=0 and lacks this particular normal-transfer\nobstruction. It still needs the appropriate constituent moment distribution\nunless row constraints explicitly enforce the common section field.\n\n## Small-strain and discrete-map limits\n\nAt e=0 the largest material strain magnitude is h*k/2: 0.05236% at 30deg and\n0.10472% at 60deg. At e=0.002 it is (e+h/2-zbar)*k: 0.13963% and 0.27925%.\nThus the classical small-strain/large-rotation premise is reasonable for linear\nelastic material. It is not a proof that every alternative finite-strain stress\nmeasure, shear theory or discretization differs by less than 1%. In particular,\nthe 60deg eccentric elastic stress at the beam outer fiber is about 586 MPa;\nan S355 plastic material would invalidate this linear elastic oracle.\n\nThe inspected corotational path removes rigid rotation before calling native\nelement response (corotational.py::_corotational_deformation_state and\n::corotational_work_conjugate_response). Its centroid extension and local\ncurvature therefore have the required engineering-strain, small-local-rotation\ncontinuum interpretation; this review does not establish a finite-mesh error\nbound or certify every native element constitutive path.\n\nThe retained map is\n\n    ub=sum(w_i*us_i)+(R(sum(w_i*theta_i))-I)*e_vector,\n    theta_b=sum(w_i*theta_i).\n\nOn this planar single-axis family, linear interpolation of rotation vectors is\nexact for phi=k*s and has no noncommuting-rotation error. Require partition of\nunity and reproduction of the beam attachment's reference x coordinate.\nInterpolation of translations between shell stations samples a chord rather\nthan the exact circle; the arc-chord position error is O(k*hs^2). Thus the\nnonmatching discrete map is not exactly the continuum arc even when its\nrotation is exact. Matching meshes also approximate arcs by element chords.\nThese are legitimate convergence errors to measure, not permission to derive\nthe oracle from the production map, adjust loads to the observed solution, or\nrelax the specified tolerances.\n\n### Prospective 1.5-ratio chord check\n\nThere is a particularly important deterministic refinement risk for nb=1.5*nx.\nLet h=L/nx, delta=k*h, sinc(a)=sin(a)/a. Sample the analytical cylinder at shell\nstations, interpolate its shell translations linearly, and use exact linear\nsingle-axis rotation interpolation for beam nodes. Every two shell elements\ncontain three beam elements, at s/h=0,2/3,4/3,2. Set\n\n    C=lambda_s*sinc(delta/2), D=e*k*sinc(delta/3).\n\nThe shell chord stretch is C. For the two outer beam elements the exact chord\nstretch on that candidate state is\n\n    lambda_outer=sqrt(C^2+D^2+2*C*D*cos(delta/6));\n\nfor the central beam element crossing a shell vertex it is\n\n    lambda_mid=lambda_s*sinc(delta)+D.\n\nConsequently the mean beam-minus-shell chord stretch is\n\n    Delta=[2*lambda_outer+lambda_mid]/3-C,\n\nwhere the continuum centroid stretch difference is e*k. At leading order in\ndelta and small e*k,\n\n    Delta-e*k = -lambda_s*delta^2/24 + O(e*k*delta^2,delta^4).\n\nThe first term is a *differential* axial-strain error, which common axial motion\ncannot remove. Using the exact formulas above at Phi=60deg, e=0.002 m:\n\n|nx|Delta-e*k|bias divided by e*k|\n|---:|---:|---:|\n|8|-7.1392985830e-4|-34.0876%|\n|16|-1.7862573077e-4|-8.5288%|\n|32|-4.4665390427e-5|-2.1326%|\n\nHere e*k=0.0020943951024 and lambda_s=0.9996509341496. The 16-to-32 change in\nthis bias alone is about 6.40% of the intended eccentric centroid strain\ndifference. At e=0 the same interpolation creates a nonzero mean differential\nchord strain despite zero continuum centroid strain difference.\n\nThis is an independent geometric candidate-state check, not a prediction of the\nnonlinear equilibrium error: actual FE energy, shears, local curvature and row\nmotion influence the response. In particular, 2.13% strain bias must not simply\nbe reported as 2.13% tip error. However, eccentric coupling contributes 14/17.15\nof the composite rigidity, so this is consequential and provides no prospective\nbasis to assume the requested 1% analytical/0.25% refinement gates will pass at\nnx=32. The matching case has a common chord factor and lacks this leading\ndifferential term. If the nonmatching case fails, distinguish interpolation\ndiscretization from the now-explicit continuum reference and from solver\nequilibrium defects. Keep the gate open and preserve evidence; do not fit the\nreference, alter physical inputs or silently add refinements beyond authority.\n\nComparing only the tip rotation when the tip rotation was prescribed would be\ntautological. Prefer the force/moment terminal prescription above and compare\nboth shell and beam tip displacements and common rotation. Record whether errors\nare componentwise or Euclidean before observing results; avoid percent errors\nfor reference-zero components.\n\n## Scalar reference values\n\nPositive My/ry convention above, displacements in m, moment in N m, force in N.\n\n|e|Phi|M total|Nb=-Ns|shell ux|shell uz|beam ux|beam uz|\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n|0|30deg|1.6493361431|0|-0.0450703414|-0.2558726308|-0.0450703414|-0.2558726308|\n|0|60deg|3.2986722863|0|-0.1730066569|-0.4774648293|-0.1730066569|-0.4774648293|\n|0.002|30deg|8.9797190015|3665.1914292|-0.0452370081|-0.2558279726|-0.0442370081|-0.2560959218|\n|0.002|60deg|17.9594380030|7330.3828584|-0.1732953320|-0.4772981626|-0.1715632812|-0.4782981626|\n\nOwn centroid moments are independent of e: at 30deg ms=0.9162978573,\nmb=0.7330382858 N m; at 60deg both double. Reversing the bend must reverse\nrotation and loads consistently; the eccentric axial stretch changes sign too,\nso merely negating uz while keeping the same radius is not the signed solution.\n\n## Required adoption before analytical acceptance\n\n1. Explicitly adopt the constrained cylindrical-strip scope and row constraints.\n2. Fix rectangle orientation, probe identity, elastic material and strain law.\n3. Adopt constituent end forces/moments and terminal-only dead-load equivalence.\n4. Keep 1% analytical and 0.25% refinement gates unchanged, with declared error\n   normalization; assess actual FE evidence independently.\n\nThese clarify the benchmark physics; they do not add numerical execution\nauthority, establish qualification or prove acceptance of the implementation.\n\nThe referenced Abaqus benchmark supports the classical single-beam pure-moment\nrelation ML/EI=2*pi*n and illustrates end moments/force couples. It does not\nestablish the eccentric free-width shell/beam reduction made here. Source:\n[Abaqus geometrically nonlinear cantilever benchmark](https://docs.software.vt.edu/abaqusv2025/English/SIMACAEBMKRefMap/simabmk-c-nlgeocantilever.htm),\nLoading and Results sections, accessed 2026-10-18. The composite extension and\ncounterexample above are this review's independent derivation.\n", "numLines": 293, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "293 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\.development\\nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017\\supervisor_reference_review.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
