{"id": "reasoning-3bec2cd7-bf9b-545b-b958-213c22fb0dc4-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346772436, "updatedAt": 1792346772436, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now I understand the structure. For R3: in the `n_red == 0` branch, `fully_constrained_displacement` is chosen as:\n- `initial_committed_displacement.copy()` if checkpoint_resume\n- `initial_affine_offset` if force_restart\n- raw `u0` otherwise\n\nThe fix: construct the full constrained vector through the map before all evaluation/recovery/return uses. So when rotating_mpc_map is not None, apply kinematics to whichever linear vector is selected. Note `initial_committed_displacement` is already mapped (in the else branch at the end: `initial_committed_displacement = rotating_mpc_map.apply_kinematics(...)`). But the n_red==0 branch uses `initial_affine_offset` (linear) or `u0` (linear). So the fix: \n\n```python\nif n_red == 0:\n    if checkpoint_resume:\n        fully_constrained_displacement = initial_committed_displacement.copy()\n    else:\n        linear_vec = initial_affine_offset if force_restart else np.asarray(u0, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n        fully_constrained_displacement = (\n            rotating_mpc_map.apply_kinematics(linear_vec)\n            if rotating_mpc_map is not None\n            else linear_vec\n        )\n```\n\nWait — but for force_restart, `initial_affine_offset` is `force_exact_base_offset` which is an offset, not a full displacement... Hmm. Actually for force_restart with n_red==0, T has zero columns, so `T@q` is empty; `initial_committed_displacement = projected_free + force_exact_base_offset` where projected_free is zeros(total_dofs). So initial_affine_offset = force_exact_base_offset = supplied_linear - projected_free = supplied_linear. So it is the full supplied linear displacement. OK.\n\nActually simpler: in all cases, `initial_committed_displacement` is already the mapped full vector. For the non-checkpoint branches, is `initial_committed_displacement` equal to mapped(u0 or affine offset)? In the force branch with initial_displacements None: initial_committed_displacement = T@q (q empty → zeros) then mapped. And initial_affine_offset = zeros. Hmm, so mapped(zeros) vs zeros — for n_red==0 with no rotations... apply_kinematics on zeros gives zeros + rotation correction at zero rotation = zeros. Actually theta_bar=0 → delta_R = R(0)-I - skew? Let me check `_nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation`: at theta=0, R(0)=I, and the linear part is... the linearized offset is theta_bar x r_0 = skew(theta)@r_0. Let me check the function.\n\nActually the cleanest R3 fix consistent with the review: \"Construct the full constrained vector through the map before all evaluation/recovery/return uses, preserving the existing branch's status semantics.\" So:\n\n```python\nfully_constrained_displacement = (\n    initial_committed_displacement.copy()\n    if checkpoint_resume\n    else (\n        rotating_mpc_map.apply_kinematics(initial_affine_offset if force_restart else np.asarray(u0, ...))\n        if rotating_mpc_map is not None\n        else (initial_affine_offset if force_restart else np.asarray(u0, ...))\n    )\n)\n```\n\nBut wait — for force_restart, initial_affine_offset is the linear supplied displacement; mapping gives the correct nonlinear physical state. For the plain case, u0 is linear; mapping gives the finite rotated lever arm. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety: for force_restart, `initial_committed_displacement` is already mapped(supplied_linear). And initial_affine_offset = force_exact_base_offset = supplied_linear (since projected_free=0). So mapped(initial_affine_offset) == initial_committed_displacement. Consistent.\n\nFor the non-restart case: initial_committed_displacement = mapped(T@q + u0) = mapped(u0) since q empty. So mapped(u0) == initial_committed_displacement. So actually in ALL three branches, `initial_committed_displacement` equals the correct mapped vector! So the simplest correct fix: always use `initial_committed_displacement.copy()`.\n\nWait, is that right? checkpoint_resume: initial_committed_displacement is mapped and verified equal to supplied. force_restart: mapped(projected_free + offset) = mapped(supplied_linear). plain: mapped(T@q+u0) = mapped(u0). Yes — in the n_red==0 case, all three give the same as mapping the branch's linear choice. So the fix is to use `initial_committed_displacement.copy()` unconditionally. That's minimal and correct. But careful: is `initial_committed_displacement` always defined at that point? Yes, set in all branches above.\n\nHmm, but wait: does the review's concern \"This also risks pairing initially activated mapped state with uncorrected final recovery\" — the fully constrained branch uses `fully_constrained_displacement` for evaluation and recovery. Using the mapped vector fixes it.\n\nLet me double check the plain (non-force, non-checkpoint) branch: `else:` (control_name != \"force\") — q = _reduced_coordinates(T, u0, initial_linear_displacements); initial_affine_offset = u0; initial_committed_displacement = T@q + u0, then mapped. With n_red=0, T@q is zeros. So mapped(u0). Good.\n\nSo R3 fix: replace the branch selection with `fully_constrained_displacement = initial_committed_displacement.copy()`. But hold on — is that exactly \"preserving the existing branch's status semantics\"? Yes, status semantics unchanged.\n\nActually wait, one more check: for force_restart, is initial_affine_offset == supplied_linear exactly? force_exact_base_offset = _exact_binary64_additive_offset(supplied_linear, projected_free) which computes supplied_linear - projected_free exactly in binary64 such that projected_free + offset == supplied_linear exactly. With n_red=0, projected_free = T@q = zeros. So offset = supplied_linear. And initial_committed_displacement = projected_free + offset = supplied_linear, then mapped. So mapped(initial_affine_offset) == initial_committed_displacement exactly. \n\nSo the minimal fix is to use initial_committed_displacement.copy() in all cases. I'll implement that with a comment.\n\nNow R4: the tangent_request resolution. Current:\n\n```python\ntangent_request = (\n    \"work_conjugate\"\n    if rotating_mpc_present\n    and str(corotational_tangent).lower() == \"auto\"\n    and not follower_active\n    else (...)\n)\n```\n\nFix per supervisor: \"choose work_conjugate for rotating-MPC auto even when follower loads are present; retain the external follower tangent... Explicit legacy rotated/consistent requests on this new route should be refused before constitutive activation with an informative ValueError\".\n\nBut there's a conflict: later there's a check:\n```python\nif follower_active and resolved_corotational_tangent != \"consistent\":\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"Follower pressure with corotational kinematics requires corotational_tangent='consistent'.\")\n```\n\nHmm — this existing check would refuse follower + work_conjugate. Wait, but `resolve_corotational_tangent_mode(kinematics, tangent_request, follower_pressure=follower_active)` — let me check what that does. Maybe it already resolves work_conjugate → consistent when follower pressure? Let me look.", "summary": []}
