{"id": "reasoning-ccb86e19-1b12-5aa1-8579-49dc4f6464ae-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346818141, "updatedAt": 1792346818141, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Runtime: Python 3.13.9 from C:\\Python\\Python313 (not the .venv?). Interesting — the harness used sys.executable which was .venv/Scripts/python.exe but the numpy is from C:\\Python\\Python313\\Lib\\site-packages. Hmm, whatever — it works and matches supervisor's record (\"runtime is Python 3.13.9/numpy2.4.3/scipy1.16.3, baseline22+39 passed with both BLAS pools at1thread\").\n\nNote: the preflight shows numba 0.65.0 present, and CHILD_ENV sets NUMBA_DISABLE_JIT=1. The supervisor said \"Verify effective JIT/thread settings from successful runtime records, not env alone.\" So the harness fix should verify JIT is actually disabled in the child (e.g., import numba and check config.DISABLE_JIT in preflight).\n\nNow, timing: ep-attrib-cr-plastic-6k took 53s for one 6-step force-control solve on a 2x1 shell + 2 beam elements model with JIT off. That's slow. A 12-step DC solve with 40 max iterations might take ~2 minutes. That would blow the 120s diagnostic cap. Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, why is it so slow? The model is tiny (2 shell elements + 2 beam elements + 2 MPCs). 6 steps × 30 iterations × assembly... with JIT off, the plastic Q4 element return map in pure Python is slow. Each _assemble_nonlinear_system call maybe 0.2-0.5s. 6 steps × ~10 iterations = 60 assemblies ≈ 30-50s. DC 12 steps × maybe 5-10 iterations = 60-120 assemblies ≈ 60-100s. Plus half+resume+uninterrupted+cancel = 4 solves ≈ 300-400s for direct, same for interp. That's ~700s of the ~2078s non-reserve budget. Ouch. Plus mesh study and arc reference...\n\nHmm wait, but the continuation says \"Individual diagnostic<=120s\" from the original assignment. But maybe I can batch: run \"dc_full direct\" as one attempt (<=120s), \"dc_split direct\" as another (2-3 solves, might exceed 120s...). Risky.\n\nAlternative: reduce per-attempt scope so each fits in 120s: \n- dc_full direct (12 steps) ~ maybe 60-100s\n- dc_split direct: half (6 steps ~30-50s) + resume (6 steps) + uninterrupted (12 steps) — 3 solves, likely >120s. Split into separate attempts: half in one, resume+compare in another, uninterrupted in another.\n\nActually, better: write the diagnostic to save intermediate results to JSON files in the evidence dir (or a work dir), so stages can be composed across attempts. E.g., stage \"dc_half\" saves half result (displacements, element_states) via pickle/npz in a work directory; stage \"dc_resume\" loads it and resumes; stage \"dc_compare\" runs uninterrupted and compares. That fits the \"per-case flush\" requirement and keeps each attempt under 120s.\n\nBut wait — the continuation says: \"Convert these into maintained pytest cases under tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3*.py; output alone is not a test.\" So the pytest module must contain the actual assertions and run the solves itself. The final regression runs the new Slice3 modules too — with 600s timeout for the whole regression... If the Slice3 pytest module takes 700s alone, the regression will blow the 600s cap.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Maybe DC solves are faster than I fear. The preyield stage (4 solves: direct/interp × plastic/elastic, 6 steps each) took 12.6s total → ~3s per solve. So a 6-step force solve ≈ 3s?! But ep-attrib-cr-plastic-6k took 53s... Different: ep_attribution uses shell_model from ep_isolation with coupled=True... wait no, ep_attribution uses shell_model(scale, True, fz) — coupled 2x1. Hmm, but preyield in ep_edge_coupling also builds coupled 2x1/2x3 models with -2kN loads and took 12.6s for 4 solves.\n\nDifference: ep-attrib runs at -6k/-10k/-14k with cutbacks (9 cutbacks → many extra solves), and records snapshots. Also ep_attribution's model has fiber_plasticity beam CS with plasticity engaged at 6k+ (alpha_max 2.3e-2), while preyield at -2k stays elastic (fast path?). Plastic return map is the slow part. At -2k, no plasticity → fast.\n\nDC to uz=-0.20 will engage plasticity heavily (the -6k force run reached uz≈-0.177 at lambda 0.94). So DC 12 steps with plasticity: each iteration needs the plastic return map. The 6k force run: 6 steps + 9 cutbacks ≈ 15 step-solves × ~3.5s = 53s. So per Newton iteration ≈ 0.3-0.5s. DC 12 steps × ~4-8 iterations ≈ 50-100 iterations ≈ 25-50s. OK that's manageable! Half (6 steps) ≈ 15-25s, resume ≈ 15-25s, uninterrupted 12 ≈ 25-50s. Cancel ≈ 10s. Direct total ≈ 100-150s; interp similar (3 beam elements, slightly more DOFs but similar).\n\nSo per-stage attempts of <=120s each: feasible. Maybe 6-8 attempts for the EP continuation ≈ 300-500s.\n\nMesh study: R1-R4 @12k nonlinear. HANDOVER: R1/R2/R3 took 2.7/5.8/13.1s — but that was probably with JIT ON (the HANDOVER was written before the harness; the \"run time dropped to 2.7/5.8/13.1\" was after performance work, presumably JIT on). With JIT off, maybe 3-10x slower: R3 could be 40-130s. R4 (32x8) maybe 100-400s?? That would blow the budget. Hmm.\n\nWait — but is JIT actually needed? Let me check: does the repo use numba heavily in the shell element? The convergence study models are elastic (no plasticity). The earlier baseline-slice12 (22 tests incl. corotational work-conjugate tests with solves) took 67s. The test_cantilever_stiffened_shell_finite_rotation_solve (nx=2?) is small.\n\nHmm, I need actual data. The nl_small R1-R3 runs under harness haven't been done yet. Let me just try and measure. If R4@12k takes >120s, I have a problem: \"Individual diagnostic<=120s\". \n\nHmm wait, re-reading: \"Individual diagnostic<=120s, regression<=600s.\" Maybe I can treat the mesh study as one \"diagnostic\" with per-case flush and a longer timeout? No — the constraint is explicit. But also \"no exploratory numerical launch if it breaches that reserve\". The budget is the hard constraint; the 120s is per-attempt. I'll keep attempts <=120s and split cases across attempts. If a single case (R4@12k) exceeds 120s, I must report it as an unmet gate... or run it with a longer timeout? The assignment is explicit: individual diagnostic<=120s. Hmm, but M4/P4 at 140k are certainly heavier than R4 at 12k. If each case takes >120s with JIT off, the whole deliverable is impossible within the constraints. \n\nLet me look at what the harness actually enforces: `--timeout` default 120. The refusal logic only checks against budget, not a hard 120 cap. The 120s is a task constraint from the assignment. The supervisor's continuation says \"Implement and execute bounded mesh study ... with machine results, per-case flush\". Maybe per-case flush means: one attempt runs cases sequentially, printing each; if the 120s timeout kills it, partial results are preserved, then a new attempt continues remaining cases. That's the design. But a single case >120s can never complete.\n\nAlternative: check whether numba JIT is actually disabled in these runs... CHILD_ENV sets NUMBA_DISABLE_JIT=1. The original convergence numbers (2.7/5.8/13.1s) were probably with JIT enabled. The assignment requires JIT off for the final regression (\"--jit off\"), and harness runs set NUMBA_DISABLE_JIT=1. Hmm, but does the assignment require JIT off for diagnostics? \"Use evidence...\" — the harness CHILD_ENV includes NUMBA_DISABLE_JIT=1, which the initial worker chose. The supervisor verified \"both BLAS pools at 1thread\" and JIT... \"Verify effective JIT/thread settings from successful runtime records, not env alone.\"\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider: maybe numba JIT being off isn't hugely slower for these elastic models since the hot paths might be numpy-vectorized anyway. The baseline-slice12 run: 22 tests in 67s including the finite-rotation solve test. Let me look at what runtime the earlier nl runs had... there were no nl convergence runs under the harness yet.\n\nI'll just measure: first attempt = linear mesh study (fast, spsolve). Then nl_small R1-R4 in one attempt with per-case flush and 120s timeout; see how far it gets. Then continue in subsequent attempts. The per-case flush design handles it.\n\nActually — better design: make the mesh study script accept a list of case names and print each result as JSON with flush; run it with `--timeout 120` per batch. If R4 alone exceeds 120s, I'll know from the partial output (it'll print nothing for R4 and timeout). Then decision: report unmet gate or... Hmm. Actually the supervisor's findings say for the ARC reference: \"Stop if errors do not decrease or resources do not permit the next case; preserve the unmet gate.\" Similar spirit for mesh study: \"Failure leaves gate open\".\n\nOK. Now the arc reference: 24 cases (nx 8/16/32 × match/nonmatch × e 0/0.002 × 30/60deg). Each is a nonlinear elastic solve on nx×3-node shell grid + beam. nx=32 → 99 nodes × 6 dof = 594 dofs, small. Elastic (no plasticity) → faster than plastic runs. The R3@12k run (16x4) took 13.1s with JIT on; elastic arc runs at 30/60deg need more steps for convergence... maybe 10-40s each with JIT off. 24 cases × ~20s ≈ 480s. Plus potential refinements 64/128/256 (conditionally authorized if nx32 fails gates and errors decrease) — hopefully not needed, or needed only for the nonmatching eccentric case.\n\nHmm, the chord analysis predicts the nonmatching case will FAIL the 1% gate at nx32 (bias -2.13% at nx32 for e=0.002). Then refinements 64/128/256 are authorized in that order. nx=256 → 771 nodes... each solve maybe 100-300s with JIT off. That could blow everything. \"Stop if errors do not decrease or resources do not permit the next case; preserve the unmet gate.\" So if resources don't permit, preserve the unmet gate. OK.\n\nTotal estimate: EP continuation ~400s, mesh study ~400-600s, arc reference ~500-700s, final regression ~400-600s (25 modules, 600s cap, historical baseline 364 tests). Sum ≈ 1700-2300s vs ~2078 available. Very tight. I need to be efficient: batch cases per attempt, avoid reruns, get the source repairs right before running.\n\nAlso the pytest conversion: the Slice3 test module will re-run DC solves during final regression. If the module takes 300s alone, the 600s regression cap is at risk. Hmm. \"Final exact25 historical + two Slice1/2 + newSlice3 modules through portable runner\" with --timeout-seconds 600. The new Slice3 modules' DC tests are expensive. Maybe mark the expensive DC continuation tests with a slower but still real configuration? No — cannot weaken criteria. Alternatively the pytest module can use the same 12-step DC but the model is tiny; measured time will tell. Let me plan the Slice3 test module to include:\n- R1 refusal tests (cheap, no solve)\n- R3 fully-constrained mapped return (cheap)\n- R4 auto/follower dispatch + refusal (cheap inspection + maybe small solve)\n- R5 finite-state admission (small DC solves at finite restart — moderate)\n- R2 guard retention test (cheap refusal)\n- EP DC continuation: direct + interp full/split/cancel with all assertions (expensive)\n\nMaybe split into two modules: test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py (source repairs R1/R3/R4/R5 + R2 guard) and test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3_continuation.py (EP DC continuation). Both match the pattern tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3*.py.\n\nBudget-wise the final regression includes these; total regression <= 600s per the assignment. If the continuation module alone takes 200-300s, plus 25 historical modules (the historical 364-test baseline... how long? The standard regression historically maybe 300-500s?). Risky but the portable runner timeout is 600s per... let me check run_portable_ci.py --timeout-seconds semantics — probably per module or total. Let me check.\n\nLet me now check scripts/run_portable_ci.py and selection.json.", "summary": []}
