r\npytest processes. It never retries a worker, and it terminates every launched\r\nprocess tree when the shared wall-clock limit expires.\r\n\r\noptions:\r\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\r\n  --workers WORKERS\r\n  --timeout-seconds TIMEOUT_SECONDS\r\n  --matrix-shard-index MATRIX_SHARD_INDEX\r\n  --matrix-shard-count MATRIX_SHARD_COUNT\r\n  --matrix-subshard-index MATRIX_SUBSHARD_INDEX\r\n  --matrix-subshard-count MATRIX_SUBSHARD_COUNT\r\n  --development TEST [TEST ...]\r\n                        Run exactly these test files/node IDs in one source\r\n                        process; no qualification\r\n  --jit {off,on}        Focused development JIT mode (default off); use on for\r\n                        affected compiled paths\r\n  --memory-limit-mib MEMORY_LIMIT_MIB\r\n                        Focused child process-tree RSS ceiling; requires\r\n                        psutil when selected\r\n", "stderr": "", "output": "", "truncated": false}, "outputText": "usage: run_portable_ci.py [-h] [--workers WORKERS]\r\n                          [--timeout-seconds TIMEOUT_SECONDS]\r\n                          [--matrix-shard-index MATRIX_SHARD_INDEX]\r\n                          [--matrix-shard-count MATRIX_SHARD_COUNT]\r\n                          [--matrix-subshard-index MATRIX_SUBSHARD_INDEX]\r\n                          [--matrix-subshard-count MATRIX_SUBSHARD_COUNT]\r\n                          [--development TEST [TEST ...]] [--jit {off,on}]\r\n                          [--memory-limit-mib MEMORY_LIMIT_MIB]\r\n\r\nRun the current merge-test extent without reopening frozen burn-in evidence.\r\nThe S3/Q4 burn-in runner is retained as historical evidence and intentionally\r\nbinds the exact machine on which the accepted cycles ran. Ordinary pull\r\nrequests need a different property: the current merge-test inventory must run\r\non every supported host, including tests added after the burn-in closeout.\r\nThis coordinator balances whole test modules over a small number of isolated\r\npytest processes. It never retries a worker, and it terminates every launched\r\nprocess tree when the shared wall-clock limit expires.\r\n\r\noptions:\r\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\r\n  --workers WORKERS\r\n  --timeout-seconds TIMEOUT_SECONDS\r\n  --matrix-shard-index MATRIX_SHARD_INDEX\r\n  --matrix-shard-count MATRIX_SHARD_COUNT\r\n  --matrix-subshard-index MATRIX_SUBSHARD_INDEX\r\n  --matrix-subshard-count MATRIX_SUBSHARD_COUNT\r\n  --development TEST [TEST ...]\r\n                        Run exactly these test files/node IDs in one source\r\n                        process; no qualification\r\n  --jit {off,on}        Focused development JIT mode (default off); use on for\r\n                        affected compiled paths\r\n  --memory-limit-mib MEMORY_LIMIT_MIB\r\n                        Focused child process-tree RSS ceiling; requires\r\n                        psutil when selected\r\n", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Ran", "message": "cd C:/Github/ANYsolver && python scripts/run_portable_ci.py --help 2>&1 | head -40", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-b8158c38-3d54-5d83-bed0-0609ae22b101-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346830578, "updatedAt": 1792346830578, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Good. `--development` runs exactly the given test files in one source process with a shared wall-clock limit of --timeout-seconds. So the final regression = one harness attempt with timeout up to 600s (using --allow-reserve since 600 > available non-reserve? Let's see: available non-reserve = remaining - 900. If I keep remaining > 1500 at that point, a 600s attempt fits without touching reserve. I must manage budget so that final regression fits in non-reserve or use --allow-reserve since final verification IS the reserve's purpose: \"Reserve last900s for final verification\". So the final regression can use --allow-reserve.\n\nNow, timing plan. I have ~2978s total, 900 reserved for final verification (final regression + repair + handoff). So ~2078s for everything else. Must be frugal:\n\n1. Harness + prose fixes: 0s numerical.\n2. Source repairs R1/R3/R4/R5: 0s numerical, but need verification. The new pytest module will verify as part of... I need at least one focused run of the new Slice3 test module before the final regression (to avoid burning regression budget on failures). That's maybe 100-300s. Hmm. Or run only the cheap tests first (deselect the expensive continuation tests) then the full module.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: the EP DC continuation diagnostics (deliverable 2/continuation item 2) require running the solves anyway. If I write the pytest module FIRST and run it under the harness as the diagnostic evidence, I get both: the diagnostic evidence AND the maintained test. \"Convert these into maintained pytest cases under tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3*.py; output alone is not a test.\" So the pytest module IS the required evidence vehicle. The harness can run pytest with -v per test; per-case flush = pytest prints per-test results. But a 120s timeout might kill mid-module. I can run the module in pieces: first the cheap repair tests, then the continuation tests one at a time (-k selection). Each pytest attempt <=120s.\n\nBut the DC continuation tests: full 12-step + half + resume + uninterrupted + cancel for direct AND interp — as ONE test it might take 150-250s > 120s cap. Split into separate test functions: test_dc_full_direct, test_dc_split_direct (half+resume+uninterrupted), test_dc_cancel_direct, same for interp. Each <= ~120s hopefully. Hmm, test_dc_split includes 3 solves (half 6, resume 6, uninterrupted 12) ≈ 24 steps total ≈ maybe 100-150s. Risky. Could split further: half in one test... but the comparison needs all three in one process (or save/load). Within one pytest process run I can select multiple test nodes; the 120s cap applies per harness attempt, not per test. So run: attempt A: `pytest tests/test_..._continuation.py -k \"direct\" ` etc.\n\nAlternatively, structure the continuation tests to share session-scoped fixtures: half result computed once (session fixture), reused by split/cancel tests. With pytest fixtures scope=\"module\", running the whole module in one process computes half once. Then the module total = preyield(4 solves) + full(12) + half(6) + resume(6) + uninterrupted(12) + cancel(~2) ≈ 42 step-solves ≈ maybe 150-300s. Over 120s cap for a single attempt... but I can run the module under the harness with --timeout 120 and see; if it times out, preserve partial, then rerun remaining with -k. Ugly but workable. Or... hmm.\n\nWait, actually let me re-read: \"Individual diagnostic<=120s, regression<=600s.\" Maybe \"individual diagnostic\" refers to individual diagnostic runs (the attribution cases), and the mesh study/EP continuation are \"diagnostics\" too. I'll respect 120s per harness attempt for diagnostics. The final regression gets 600s.\n\nHmm, but there's a real risk the continuation module alone exceeds 120s. Options: run it via two harness attempts with -k filters. Fine.\n\nLet me now think about total numerical budget more carefully and consider trimming:\n\n- EP continuation module run(s): ~2-3 attempts × ~100s ≈ 250-300s (this doubles as diagnostic evidence AND test evidence).\n- Mesh study: linear all cases (R1-R4, M1-M4, P1-P4 + corresponding linear) — linear is cheap (~10-20s total). Nonlinear: R1-R4@12k (4 cases), M1-M4/P1-P4@140k (8 cases). The 140k cases are the expensive ones. Estimate 20-80s each with JIT off → 160-640s. Plus R4@12k maybe 30-100s. Total maybe 300-700s. Cap per attempt 120s → 3-6 attempts.\n- Arc reference: 24 cases × 10-40s ≈ 240-960s. Plus possible refinements.\n- Final regression: up to 600s (reserve).\n\nWorst case blows the budget. I must sequence so that the most important gates get evidence first, and use per-case flush so timeouts preserve partial results. Also the continuation says \"Stop optional investigations that do not change the next implementation decision.\"\n\nPriority per the continuation order: (2) EP continuation, (3) source repairs, (4) mesh study, (5) arc reference, (6) final regression. Source repairs must come before tests. Let me do:\n\nPhase A (no numerics): harness fixes, ep_attribution prose, source repairs R1/R3/R4/R5, write Slice3 test modules, write mesh study script (extend convergence_study.py with per-case JSON flush + case args), write arc reference script + analytical_derivation.md, update checkpoint.md by turn 12.\n\nPhase B (numerics, ordered):\n1. Quick source-repair verification: run the cheap Slice3 repair tests (R1/R3/R4/R5 + R2 guard) — one attempt ~60-120s.\n2. EP continuation tests (direct, interp) — 2-4 attempts.\n3. Mesh study linear (1 attempt) + nonlinear batches (3-6 attempts).\n4. Arc reference batches (4-8 attempts).\n5. Final regression (1 attempt, 600s, --allow-reserve).\n\nPhase C: HANDOVER §8, checkpoint, final handoff.\n\nNow, before writing code, I need to understand a few more source details:\n\nR5: the DC admission. Current code at 3855-3866ff:\n```python\nrow_full = displacement_control.full_row(model)\nrow_red = np.asarray(row_full @ T, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\nrow_u0 = float(row_full @ u0)\nif float(np.linalg.norm(row_red)) <= 0.0:\n    raise ValueError(\"Displacement control target is fixed or dependent and cannot be used as an unknown\")\ninitial_u = full_u(np.zeros(n_red, dtype=float))\n```\n\nFix: when rotating_mpc_map is not None, admit using the actual initial state full_u(q) and its Jacobian:\n```python\ninitial_u = full_u(q)  # actual initial state (was zeros)\nif rotating_mpc_map is not None:\n    row_jac0 = np.asarray(row_full @ rotating_mpc_map.jacobian(initial_u)).reshape(-1)\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(row_jac0)) <= 0.0:\n        raise ValueError(...)\nelse:\n    if float(np.linalg.norm(row_red)) <= 0.0:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nAnd the proportional load admission: F_prop_initial reduced at initial_u (already uses initial_u for follower; for non-follower F_prop_initial = F_prop_static and reduce_force(initial_u, ...) — the map reduce at initial_u). Currently `F_prop_red = rotating_mpc_map.reduce_force(initial_u, F_prop_initial)` — with initial_u = full_u(zeros). Fix: initial_u = full_u(q). At q with finite ry, a force on the nonlinear slave direction now has nonzero reduced force → admitted. \n\nNote: `initial_control` already uses full_u(q) when map present. Good.\n\nAlso the moving_tool branch uses full_u(q) — fine.\n\nR4: tangent_request resolution. Need to modify:\n\n```python\nrotating_mpc_present = (kinematics == \"corotational\" and has_nonlinear_rotating_mpc(model))\nif rotating_mpc_present:\n    requested_mode = str(corotational_tangent).strip().lower()\n    if requested_mode == \"auto\":\n        tangent_request = \"work_conjugate\"\n    elif requested_mode in (\"rotated\", \"consistent\"):\n        raise ValueError(\n            \"corotational_tangent='rotated'/'consistent' ... refused on the rotating-MPC route; use 'auto' or 'work_conjugate'\"\n        )\n    else:\n        tangent_request = corotational_tangent\nelse:\n    tangent_request = (existing logic)\n```\n\nWait, but what about armijo + rotating map? Existing armijo precondition: line_search='armijo' with corotational requires 'auto' or 'consistent'. With rotating map + armijo + auto → work_conjugate. The armijo check earlier only refuses 'rotated' explicitly requested. OK.\n\nAnd the follower NotImplementedError check:\n```python\nif follower_active and resolved_corotational_tangent != \"consistent\":\n    raise NotImplementedError(...)\n```\nmust become:\n```python\nif (follower_active\n        and resolved_corotational_tangent != \"consistent\"\n        and not (rotating_mpc_present and resolved_corotational_tangent == \"work_conjugate\")):\n    raise NotImplementedError(...)\n```\n\nHmm wait, but is work_conjugate + follower actually supported by the assembly? The review says follower external tangent assembly and rotating-map external reduction are present in the main loops. But there's a subtlety: `general_tangent = follower_active or ...` — fine. What about `_weighted_external_load_system` with work_conjugate? That's about external loads, independent of internal force law. And the internal force assembly: `_assemble_nonlinear_system(..., corotational_tangent=resolved)` — with \"work_conjugate\" it uses the work-conjugate pullback. OK.\n\nBut wait — there's also the check in `validate_corotational_scope` or elsewhere that might refuse work_conjugate with follower? Let me search for \"work_conjugate\" in nonlinear_static.py to see all uses.\n\nR3: fully constrained branch — use initial_committed_displacement (already mapped) unconditionally.\n\nR1: in build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map, add the fail-closed downstream check.\n\nR2: retain guard; add a refusal test (corotational + initial stress/prestrain field → NotImplementedError). The guard is at 5683–5689: \"rejects any corotational solve whose committed state contains initial stress/prestrain fields\". Let me look at that code to write the test.\n\nLet me now grep the relevant regions.", "summary": []}
