letion/target/residual records, input/source identity. The original must be preserved: copy to convergence_study_v1.py? \"Preserve original diagnostic files before replacing them\" — I'll add a NEW file diagnostics/mesh_study.py instead of modifying convergence_study.py. Cleaner.\n\n**Edit 9: diagnostics/arc_reference.py** (new) — constrained-plane-section circular arc reference FE study.\n\n**Edit 10: analytical_derivation.md** (task dir) — adopted derivation from supervisor_reference_review.md.\n\n**Edit 11: checkpoint.md** — update by turn 12 with concrete progress.\n\n**Edit 12: HANDOVER.md §8** — final update.\n\nLet me also double check the R5 example: \"master uz is fixed, ry is free, e=(0,0,e), control the beam slave uz. The linear MPC gives zero linear control row\" — wait, actually in the linear MPC, u_b,z = sum w u_s,z + (theta x r0)_z. With r0=(0,0,e), theta x r0 has z-component 0 (theta x r0 = (theta_y*e, -theta_x*e, 0)). So u_b,z = w*u_s,z. If master uz fixed... then row_red = 0 → refused. But nonlinear: u_b,z = w*u_s,z + (R(theta)-I r0)_z which depends on ry (free) → nonzero Jacobian at finite ry. Right.\n\nFor the R5 test: build the coupled cantilever, solve to a finite state (half solve), then restart DC controlling the beam slave uz with master uz fixed... hmm, need master uz fixed. Simpler test design per review: \"Add a finite-state admission test and a zero-Jacobian refusal test.\"\n\nTest A (zero-Jacobian refusal): fresh model (q=0, ry=0), fix master shell uz, control beam slave uz → linear row zero and nonlinear Jacobian at u0 also zero (ry=0) → still refused. Assert ValueError.\n\nTest B (finite-state admission): restart from a finite state where ry≠0: same control is admitted and the solve proceeds. Need: master uz fixed, ry free at finite value, control beam slave uz. Construct: solve the model first to a finite rotation state with... hmm, if master uz is fixed throughout, getting finite ry requires moment loading. Alternative: prescribe the initial state directly via initial_displacements: set shell ry=alpha at all nodes (finite), master uz=0 (fixed), and initial_displacements containing the mapped state... The DC preflight computes initial_u = full_u(q) where q from initial_displacements. The control row Jacobian at that state: row_full @ J(u). J(u) = T + delta_T where delta_T has the lever-arm correction columns. row_full picks beam slave uz. row_full@T = 0 (by construction), row_full@delta_T = the correction row = (A + r_skew) @ theta_proj ... at theta_bar with ry=alpha: A(theta,e) for e=(0,0,e): d/dtheta of (R(theta)-I)r0... The z-component derivative w.r.t. ry: -e*sin(ry) ≠ 0. So row_full@J ≠ 0 → admitted. \n\nSo test B: build model with master uz fixed (BC), beam slave defined by coupling; supply initial_displacements with finite ry (e.g., 0.5 rad) on shell nodes; DC control beam slave uz target slightly beyond current. Assert: no ValueError; solve completes; and the proportional load admission also uses full_u(q) (force on the slave uz direction: reduced force nonzero at finite state). Hmm, the load admission: F_prop_red = reduce_force(initial_u, F_prop). A force on the beam slave uz direction: reduce_force transfers f_b via delta_A.T @ f_b into slave_theta_dofs then T.T. At finite ry, nonzero. At ry=0, zero → refused. So test: fresh model with force on beam slave uz + control beam slave uz → refused (zero reduced force at ry=0); at finite ry restart → admitted.\n\nWait, but can a load be applied to a slave node? The beam node is an MPC slave; loads on slaves are fine (they get reduced). OK.\n\nAlso need to double check: with master uz fixed and initial_displacements having ry=alpha — is the supplied initial displacement compatible? The beam slave uz from the map: u_b,z = w*u_s,z + (R-I r0)_z = 0 + e*(cos(alpha)-1). The supplied initial_displacements must be the mapped physical state (to_linear_displacement is applied first, then q extracted, then full_u re-applies). The restart path: initial_linear_displacements = to_linear(u_supplied); q = _reduced_coordinates(T, u0, initial_linear). Then initial_u = full_u(q) — reconstructs mapped state. For DC restart with initial_displacements + initial_element_states + equilibrate_initial_state=False.\n\nHmm, but for a pure admission test, maybe I don't even need element states — elastic model with initial_displacements only. Is initial_displacements without initial_element_states allowed for corotational? The restart test at line 618 uses \"direct initial-displacement/material restart\". For elastic, initial_element_states might be optional. Let me not over-engineer: use the elastic coupled cantilever, initial_displacements constructed as the mapped state for a prescribed shell ry field... but wait — shell nodes with prescribed ry: are all shell DOFs free except root? The root is clamped. The initial displacement field must satisfy the constraints: root nodes zero. So set ry=alpha on non-root shell nodes... but then the beam slave nodes follow via the map. Constructing the full initial displacement vector: take q0 = vector with ry=alpha for free shell rotation dofs, u_lin = T@q0 + u0 (root fixed → u0=0), then u_mapped = apply_kinematics(u_lin). Pass u_mapped as initial_displacements.\n\nThen DC: control = beam slave uz, target = current + small increment (e.g., -0.01). num_steps=2. The solve should be admitted and complete. Also apply a proportional force on the beam slave uz direction (nonzero reduced at finite state). Hmm wait — if the proportional load is on the slave uz and control is also slave uz... that's fine for DC (lambda is unknown).\n\nActually, hmm: the DC requires \"non-zero proportional load vector\" reduced. With force on slave uz at finite ry: reduce_force gives T.T@f_mod where f_mod has slave_theta_dofs += delta_A.T @ f_b. delta_A at ry=alpha: A + r_skew. f_b = (0,0,F). delta_A.T @ f_b: A(theta,e) = d(R(theta)r0)/dtheta... the third column of A.T dotted... anyway nonzero at alpha≠0. Good.\n\nFor the refusal test: same model, fresh state (no initial displacements), same control/load → ValueError at admission (both row and load checks give zero at ry=0; row check fires first).\n\nNow R3 test: \"prescribe a rigid rotation ry=alpha to all shell nodes with translations (R-I)X, constrain all remaining master DOFs, leave beam slaves defined only by the coupling, then call the fully constrained public route. Assert finite beam offsets/rotations and zero strain for the rigid motion... Cover direct and interpolated descriptors... do not require an ordinary completed-load status where the existing contract uses empty_reduced_system.\"\n\nBuild: shell strip (nx=2, ny=1) + beam via coupling; BCs: ALL shell dofs prescribed: root... wait \"clamp root only\" is for the arc reference. For R3: prescribe every shell/master DOF (all shell nodes: ux,uy,uz,rx,ry,rz prescribed with the rigid motion values), so all shell DOFs are fixed; beam DOFs are slaves of the coupling → n_red = 0. Prescribed values: rigid rotation ry=alpha about... a rigid rotation about the y-axis through some point: translations (R-I)X where X is node position relative to rotation center. Take rotation about origin: u = (R(ry)-I) @ X for translations, rotations = (0,alpha,0).\n\nThen the beam slave must obey the finite rotated lever arm: u_b = sum w u_s + (R(theta_bar)-I) r0. Since all masters prescribed with the rigid motion, theta_bar = (0,alpha,0), u_s_bar = (R-I)X_attach. Expected u_b = (R-I)(X_attach + r0)... wait: u_b = (R-I)X_attach + (R-I)r0 = (R-I)(X_attach + r0) = (R-I)X_b. And theta_b = (0,alpha,0). So the beam node undergoes the same rigid motion. Zero strain.\n\nCurrent buggy code returns u0-based (linearized) values: u_b,z from u0 = prescribed linear MPC value: u0[slave] = resolved value = sum w*u_s + theta_bar x r0 (linear lever arm) = (R-I)X_b linearized ≈ alpha x r0... The assertion: result.displacements at beam slave dofs equals the exact finite rigid motion values, and element strains ~0.\n\nHow to check \"zero strain\"? The result with n_red=0 goes through _assemble_nonlinear_system with the fully constrained displacement (when has_initial_fields or requires_native_tl...). Hmm — for a plain elastic model without initial fields, the n_red==0 branch does NOT assemble (only if has_initial_fields or requires_native_tl_evaluation). It just returns the displacement with status empty_reduced_system. So the test asserts the returned displacements satisfy the finite-rotation map exactly (and the linearized map does NOT hold). Strain check: compute _assemble_nonlinear_system on the returned u and check F_int ≈ 0? For a rigid motion, internal force should be ~0. That's a good zero-strain check. Let me do that: F_int norm small relative to a reference (e.g., E*A*something). Actually corotational rigid motion → F_int ≈ 0 up to numerical noise. I'll assert norm < 1e-6 * scale.\n\nAlso cover direct and interpolated descriptors (parametrize route).\n\nAlso assert status: result.status == \"empty_reduced_system\"? What's the status string... info[\"stop_reason\"]=\"empty_reduced_system\"; status likely \"empty_reduced_system\" too. The review says \"do not require an ordinary completed-load status where the existing contract uses empty_reduced_system\". I'll assert stop_reason == \"empty_reduced_system\".\n\nR4 tests:\n1. test_auto_with_follower_selects_work_conjugate: coupled model + follower pressure load (tiny pressure on a shell element) + dead nodal load; solve with corotational_tangent=\"auto\" → result.info[\"corotational_tangent\"] == \"work_conjugate\" and info[\"follower_pressure\"] is True. Also small-pressure limit: another solve with pressure → 0 (or follower_pressure=False) also work_conjugate — \"ensure internal-force dispatch does not jump as pressure tends to zero\": compare info[\"corotational_tangent\"] for pressure 1e-6 vs no pressure: both \"work_conjugate\".\n2. test_explicit_legacy_modes_refused_on_rotating_route: corotational_tangent=\"consistent\" and \"rotated\" → ValueError. And legacy models WITHOUT rotating descriptor retain explicit semantics: a shell-only corotational model with corotational_tangent=\"consistent\" + follower → works (resolved \"consistent\"), and \"rotated\" without follower → works. \n3. Complete derivative check at finite state with follower: reduced net internal-minus-external force derivative vs central differences including external load derivatives and MPC geometric contribution. This is the heavy one. Implementation: model coupled cantilever + follower pressure on a shell element + dead nodal loads. At a finite state q: \n   r_net(q) = J^T (F_int(u(q)) - F_ext(u(q), lambda=1)) where F_ext includes follower pressure at current area (position-dependent). The reduced tangent used by the solver: K_red = J^T (K_T - K_ext) J + K_mpc,geom. FD: d/dq of reduce_force(u(q), F_int(u(q)) - F_ext(u(q))). Need _weighted_external_load_system(model, terms, u, tangent=False) for follower force at state u. Let me check its signature... it's used in the DC loop: `_weighted_external_load_system(model, [proportional_term], u, tangent=False)`. The proportional_term is (load_case, factor). So F_ext(u) = _weighted_external_load_system(model, [(load_case, 1.0)], u, tangent=False)[0]... it returns (F, K)? In DC loop: `F_prop_current, K_prop = _weighted_external_load_system(model, [proportional_term], u, tangent=True)`. With tangent=False presumably returns (F, None) or (F, 0-matrix). \n\n   FD of r_net(q) = reduce_force(u(q), F_int(u(q)) - F_ext(u(q))) w.r.t q, compare against reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int-F_ext, K_ext). This mirrors the existing tangent FD test but with external load dependence included. \n\nR1 tests:\n- refusal: coupled model + extra node C + equation uz_C = uz_B (B = beam slave node). How to add a generic equation? model.constraint_equations — ConstraintEquation(terms, rhs, source_id). Let me check the API: `ConstraintEquation(terms=((dof, coef), ...), rhs=..., source_id=...)` wi