{"id": "reasoning-2c3b9174-ec7b-5259-997d-2a6280a53424-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792346873880, "updatedAt": 1792346873880, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "I see: DC has no cutbacks — a failed step stops the solve (status stopped_at_limit/diverged), snapshots only appended after accepted steps. So \"rejected-step snapshots must exclude rejected history\" is structurally satisfied; the test should verify: in a run where a step fails (e.g., max_iterations too small), snapshots == accepted steps only, and the returned displacement equals the last accepted state (rollback: q = q_step_start on failure... wait, on failure `break` happens after rollback — q = q_step_start, so u_final = last accepted state. Good.)\n\nAlso progress_callback: emit_progress with event_type \"nonlinear_static_step\", stage \"nonlinear_static.displacement\". Cancellation: `cancellation_safe_point(cancellation_token, ...)` at each step start. So cancel after two accepted steps via progress_callback → token.cancel → next step's safe point raises SolveCancelled. \n\nNow, for the cancellation test: \"Cancel after two accepted increments; compare deep copies of initial displacement/state inputs and saved accepted snapshots; reusing those same inputs must reproduce the fresh resume.\" And \"If cancellation throws before exposing the cancelled run's local snapshots, a focused test can capture _increment_snapshot outputs without changing mechanics\" — the cancelled run raises SolveCancelled so no result object; we can't inspect its snapshots. At minimum: after cancellation, the caller's restart inputs (the deep-copied half displacements/states) are unchanged, and reusing them reproduces the fresh resume.\n\nWait, more precisely from the addendum: \"Retain deep before/after copies of supplied displacement, every nested material history array/metadata, and half accepted snapshots. Prove unchanged after a successful resume, a deliberately rejected resume and token cancellation; then reuse the same untouched input for a clean successful resume.\"\n\n- successful resume: inputs unchanged after.\n- deliberately rejected resume: how? A resume that fails (e.g., impossible target or tiny max_iterations → step fails) → returns result with failure status (not exception); inputs must remain unchanged. E.g., resume with max_iterations=1 → first step fails → status stopped_at_limit/diverged; inputs unchanged.\n- token cancellation: raises SolveCancelled; inputs unchanged.\n- then reuse same inputs → clean successful resume equals the fresh resume result.\n\nOK. Now — the \"rejected resume\" concern from the addendum: \"if an ordinary resumed solve rejects its first new step, its rolled-back displacement/history can be the valid half state while the returned lambda remains the helper's zero initial guess.\" Fine, we just check input immutability, not lambda.\n\nNow, the split comparison: \"Stitch half+resume accepted control/load/alpha histories and compare with the twelve uninterrupted steps.\" Resume result has 6 new records (step_index 1-6 local? with step_index_offset=0). The half solve's achieved -0.10 can differ from exact target by constraint tolerance. Compare: resumed final u vs uninterrupted final u within existing restart tolerances; lambda within tolerance; plastic history (alpha arrays) within tolerance.\n\nWhat are \"existing restart tolerances\"? The Slice1/2 restart test (line 618) compares... let me look at the end of that test to see the tolerances used.", "summary": []}
