ntermediate plastic states → different final states at first order? Hmm, but actually both end at uz=-0.20 (within 1e-8). The final equilibrium at uz=-0.20 is determined by the constitutive history, which depends on the path (increment sequence). With 6+6 vs 12 steps, the intermediate states differ (e.g., after 6 steps: -0.10 vs -0.1666 in uninterrupted? No wait — uninterrupted step 6 target = -0.10 too! 12 steps to -0.20: step k target = -0.20*k/12 = -0.0167k. Step 6 = -0.10. Same as half! And steps 7-12 targets: -0.1167, -0.1333, ..., -0.20. Resume: initial = half achieved (≈-0.10 within constraint tolerance 1e-8), then 6 steps to -0.20: targets = initial + (target_total - initial)*k/6 = -0.10 - 0.10*k/6 → -0.1167, -0.1333, ..., -0.20. Same sequence (up to the half achieved-vs-target error ≤1e-8 and floating differences)!\n\nSo the target sequences coincide: half 6 steps = uninterrupted steps 1-6; resume 6 steps = uninterrupted steps 7-12 (up to O(1e-8) offset in starting point). The Newton iterates won't be bit-identical (different starting lambda guess: 0 vs 0.5·lambda... wait, resume starts lam=0 vs uninterrupted step 7 starts at lam_6). Converged solutions per step should agree to within the convergence tolerance (1e-8 relative residual). The final state difference will be governed by accumulated convergence tolerance ~1e-8·scale, not 1e-10. So rtol 1e-8/atol 1e-10 on displacements may be too tight. Hmm.\n\nThe existing restart test used force control with num_steps=2+2 vs 4 and tolerance 1e-10, and it passes with rtol 1e-8. There, each step converges to residual 1e-10, so state differences ~1e-10ish. Here tolerance is 1e-8 (DC_PARAMS), so per-step equilibrium error ~1e-8 relative → displacement differences maybe ~1e-9..1e-8 absolute (uz scale 0.2). atol 1e-10 would fail. I should use tolerances consistent with the solver tolerance: e.g., assert allclose(resumed.displacements, uninterrupted.displacements, rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-8)? The continuation says \"compare u/lambda/history with uninterrupted using existing restart tolerances, no relaxation\". \"Existing restart tolerances\" = the 1e-8/1e-10 from the Slice1/2 test. Hmm, but that test used tolerance=1e-10 solves. Here the assignment fixes tolerance=1e-8 for DC. If the comparison at 1e-8/1e-10 fails, is that a gate failure or wrong tolerance choice? \n\nLet me think about the actual error magnitude. Per DC step, convergence requires residual_norm <= tolerance * reference AND constraint_error <= tolerance * target_scale. tolerance=1e-8, target_scale = max(|target_increment|, |target|, 1e-9) = 0.2. So constraint error <= 2e-9 m per step. The equilibrium residual tolerance 1e-8 relative → force residual ~1e-8 * ref. The resulting state error per step ~ ||K^-1|| * residual. The difference between resumed and uninterrupted final states: both satisfy their own step equilibria; the path difference comes from (a) half achieved vs exact -0.10 (≤2e-9 offset in control value, propagating to ~1e-8-ish state differences?), (b) different Newton trajectories converging to slightly different points within tolerance.\n\nRealistically the difference could be ~1e-8..1e-7 in displacements. rtol=1e-8, atol=1e-10 would likely FAIL. I'll use scaled tolerances: assert max abs diff <= 1e-8 (absolute, matching the control tolerance scale) for displacements? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: \"using existing restart tolerances\" — I'll implement the comparison with the existing rtol=1e-8/atol=1e-10 BUT if it fails numerically, that's evidence... no wait, I can't iterate on budget. Let me think harder about what the actual difference will be.\n\nThe resume reconstructs q from the half displacements via inverse map + LSQR, then re-applies the map. The reconstruction is exact (the code guarantees exact reconstruction: initial_committed_displacement must equal supplied exactly for checkpoint; for direct initial_displacements, force_exact_base_offset ensures exact reconstruction). So the resume star