olerance... but the continuation explicitly says \"using existing restart tolerances, no relaxation\". The \"existing restart tolerances\" in the repo are rtol 1e-8/atol 1e-10. Ugh.\n\nLet me look at what tolerance the DC actually achieves in practice — the earlier receipt ep-edge-preyield or the DC attempt... the DC full run failed on checkpoint emission before producing output. No data.\n\nDecision: I'll write the test with the existing rtol=1e-8, atol=1e-10 for displacements, and if the measured diff exceeds it, I'll report the actual diff and adjust the assertion to a justified tolerance tied to the solver's convergence tolerance (documented, not silently relaxed). Actually — better: make the test compute and assert with the existing tolerances; run; if fails by margin, examine the actual numbers and make a principled choice, documenting it in the handoff. The budget cost: the continuation tests run once or twice.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider the lambda guess issue. The addendum says: \"The public helper call supplies initial_load_factor=0... Writing dlambda=lambda_h+dLambda makes it the same first displacement equation as starting with lambda_h, up to the half solve's residual tolerance.\" So the first resumed step converges to the same equilibrium as uninterrupted step 7 (same target). The converged point is determined by target + equilibrium; Newton converges to the unique solution near the start regardless of the initial lambda guess (as long as it converges). So the per-step states should agree to Newton convergence accuracy of EACH solve. Both accept states with residual <= 1e-8*ref. The difference between the two accepted states is bounded by ~2x the state error corresponding to that residual: ~2e-4 N / K_eff. K_eff for this model: uz 0.2 m under ~6-8 kN → K ~ 4e4 N/m. State error ~ 1e-4/4e4 = 2.5e-9 m per step. Accumulated over 6 steps (path divergence grows): maybe ~1e-8 m. So displacements diff ~ 1e-8. atol 1e-10 fails, rtol 1e-8 (→ 2e-9 for uz) fails marginally.\n\nAlternatively — the plastic history difference: alpha ~2.3e-2; relative diff maybe 1e-7-ish.\n\nI'll write the test with explicit tolerances: displacements atol=1e-8, rtol=1e-7; load_factor atol=1e-6; alpha atol=1e-9, rtol=1e-5 — justified as \"existing restart comparison tolerances scaled to the DC convergence tolerance 1e-8\". Hmm, but \"no relaxation\"...\n\nActually, re-reading the continuation: \"resume6steps to-.20 with deep copied initial_displacements/initial_element_states, equilibrate_initial_state=False; uninterrupted12steps... assert accepted residual<=1e-7, control target<=1e-8m, positive plastic strain, constraints, full force/wrench reactions, input immutability, split history tolerances.\" — \"split history tolerances\" is vague, left to me. The original assignment: \"compare u/lambda/history with uninterrupted using existing restart tolerances, no relaxation\". I'll use rtol=1e-8/atol=1e-10 as primary; if the run shows it's marginally exceeded, I'll document the measured diff and set the assertion to the tightest tolerance that the measured evidence supports with a clear justification (this is a test-design choice, not a physics tolerance). Let me first write with the existing tolerances and see.\n\nHmm, actually there's a smarter approach to reduce path divergence: the half solve and uninterrupted steps 1-6 are bit-identical computations (same targets, same tolerances? No — target_scale differs: 0.1 vs 0.2 → constraint tolerance 1e-9 vs 2e-9). To make them identical I could... no, must keep 6 steps to -0.10 and 12 steps to -0.20 as specified.\n\nOK, enough analysis. Let me also figure out the \"full force/wrench reactions\" assertion: support reactions must balance the applied loads: sum of support reaction forces + applied total force ≈ 0, and total moment including deformed force arms ≈ 0. The support_reactions from _support_reaction_resultants: \"sums additive rotation covectors; do not label those raw sums spatial moments in a general finite-rotation configuration without the appropriate conversion.\" For the EP model (planar bending, moments about y), the reaction moment covector for theta=(0,phi,0) equals the spatial moment about y exactly (per the reference review: J_l(theta)*ey = ey). So I can check: root reaction force + total applied force ≈ 0 (3 components), and root reaction moment (covector) + applied moment about the root ≈ 0. Applied loads are pure forces at tip shell nodes (no applied moments): the reaction moment should balance the moment of the applied forces about the root: sum r_i × F_i with r_i the CURRENT positions? Hmm — careful: the support reaction \"moment\" from _support_reaction_resultants is in what sense? For an equilibrium check, the cleanest independent check is the reduced residual (already asserted) plus total force balance. For the wrench: the internal force imbalance transferred to masters gives reaction resultants; the moment balance in the covector sense: reaction_moment_covector should equal -(sum over applied forces of J_l(theta_i)^T (r_i × F))? This is getting deep. \n\nThe existing Slice1/2 test checked \"total support force\" balance only, and the review noted the moment wasn't checked. The continuation asks for \"full force/wrench reactions\". Pragmatic approach: \n1. Force balance: root_reaction[:3] + lambda * total_applied_force ≈ 0.\n2. Moment balance about the root in the y-covector sense: for planar bending (all rotations about y, forces along z at shell nodes), the spatial moment about y from applied forces = sum (x_i * F_z,i) with x_i current x positions. The reaction covector p_ry relates to spatial moment m_y via p = J_l(theta)^T m; for theta=(0,phi,0), p_ry = m_y. So assert root_reaction[4] + m_y_applied ≈ 0 where m_y_applied = sum over loaded nodes of (current_x * F_z) (moment about y of forces at current positions, about the root point... need the reference point of the reaction moment — presumably the origin or the constraine