ce of the curved state, which the DC step must resolve... The augmented system solves K dq - f dlambda = -r(q). The residual includes the internal force imbalance of the initial field. Newton should handle it if it's not too large. alpha=0.4 rad uniform curvature over 1m: curvature ~0.4; M ~ E*I*0.4... shell strip 0.2 wide, t=0.02: I = 0.2*0.02^3/12=1.33e-8 m^4... wait bending about y (curvature in x-z plane): the shell bends as a plate in the x-z plane: bending stiffness per unit width D = Et^3/12(1-nu^2) = 2.1e11*8e-9/12.91 ≈ 0.13 N·m; times width 0.2 → 0.026 N·m². M = 0.026*0.4 ≈ 0.01 N·m. Tiny. Plus the beam stiffener: Iy=5.33e-9? CS Iy = 0.01*0.04^3/12 = 5.33e-9 m^4 → E*I = 1120 N·m² → M = 448 N·m. Hmm, that's a big moment. The DC step must dissipate this via lambda... the proportional load is only 5e3 N on the slave. The augmented system: the initial residual r(q0) = J^T(F_int - 0*F_ext) = J^T F_int — magnitude ~ hundreds of N (moment/lever). Newton: K dq - f dlambda = -r. The control row constrains dq. Is there a solution? The system is solvable as long as the augmented matrix is nonsingular. The step might need several iterations but should converge since it's elastic. Risk: with only 2 steps and 30 iterations, probably fine. But the target -0.01 is close to initial -0.00397; the step is small while the initial residual is large — the first Newton iteration must find dq that both controls uz and balances the residual... The residual has components orthogonal to the control row; Newton solves the full augmented system — it will find dq (large rotation change) + dlambda to satisfy both equations. It should converge to the equilibrium with control value = target. OK, plausible. If it fails, I'll adjust (e.g., equilibrate... no, can't equilibrate — that would change the state). Alternative: make the initial state an actual equilibrium: solve a force-control case first to reach a finite state, then restart DC. That's more robust and matches the review's \"finite restart\" scenario. But the master uz is fixed in this model... a force-control solve on this model: loads on slave uz direction; at ry=0 the reduced load is zero → force control would refuse too (\"non-zero proportional load\"?). Hmm.\n\nActually simpler robust approach: don't fix ALL shell uz; the R5 scenario needs \"master uz fixed\" only for the coupling whose slave we control. The tip beam node's master is shell node (2,0). Fix only that node's uz (and root). Then the linear control row for the tip beam slave uz = w*(uz columns of master (2,0)) = 0 (fixed) → zero linear row. Nonlinear at finite ry: nonzero. And the model is a normal cantilever otherwise; a force-control pre-solve can reach a finite state (bending under tip load on... wait, the proportional load must be on the slave uz direction for the load-admission part. If I put the load on the slave node, force control at fresh state: reduced load zero → refused. So pre-solve with a DIFFERENT load (e.g., tip moment or force on free shell nodes) to reach finite ry, then restart DC with the slave-uz load.\n\nHmm, but wait: if the pre-solve load is on shell nodes, then at the finite state the DC admission reduces the slave-uz load: reduce_force(u, F_prop) — f_b on slave uz → transferred via delta_A.T → theta dofs → nonzero at finite ry. Good.\n\nLet me restructure the R5 finite-state test:\n1. Build model: root clamp + fix uz ONLY on shell node (2,0) (the master of the tip beam slave). Load A (pre-solve): tip transverse load on shell node (1,0) or (2,1)... wait (2,0) uz is fixed; use node (2,1) (the other tip node) with Fz. Solve force control to finite rotation (ry at tip ~0.3+ rad). \n2. From that result, restart DC: control = beam tip slave uz, target = current - 0.005, load B = slave-uz force (proportional). initial_displacements=result.displacements, initial_element_states=result.element_states, equilibrate_initial_state=False.\n3. Assert completed and target achieved.\n\nThis is a genuine \"finite restart\" admission — exactly the R5 scenario. And the refusal test: fresh model, same DC → ValueError.\n\nWait, but for the refusal test, the load must also be the slave-uz load (both row and load admission fail at ry=0; row fires first). Fine.\n\nBut hold on — the pre-solve: with master (2,0) uz fixed, does force control work? Load on node (2,1) Fz: reduced load nonzero (free dofs). Yes.\n\nHmm, also: is the tip beam slave's linear control row really zero? row_full = e_row for beam slave uz. T row for slave uz: from the MPC u_b,z = u_s,z (w=1, master (2,0)) + (theta×r0)_z = u_s,z + 0. So T[slave_uz, :] = T[master_uz_row-ish]... master uz is FIXED → no column for it → T row = 0. Yes zero.\n\nAnd at finite state: J = T + delta_T; row_full@J = row_full@delta_T (since row_full@T=0). delta_T row for slave uz = (A + r_skew) @ theta_proj restricted... theta_proj = T[slave_theta rows, :] — slave theta row of T: theta_b = theta_s (master rotations) → T[slave_theta, cols] = T[master_theta...]. Master (2,0) rotations are free (only uz fixed) → theta_proj nonzero. (A+r_skew) at theta_bar=(0,ry,0): A = d/dtheta[(R-I)r0]. The z-row of A w.r.t. ry: d/dry[(cos ry - 1)*e] = -e sin(ry) ≠ 0. So row_full@J ≠ 0 at finite ry. \n\nNow, also the load admission in the restart: F_prop_red = reduce_force(full_u(q), F_prop) ≠ 0 at finite state. With the OLD code (full_u(zeros)): at q=0, ry=0 → zero → refused. So the test discriminates both halves of R5. \n\nLet me also reconsider: after the pre-solve, is ry at the master (2,0) actually nonzero? The pre-solve bends the strip; tip rotation nonzero. Yes.\n\nOne more subtlety: the DC restart uses initial_load_factor=0 (Newton guess). The pre-solve load A is NOT the DC proportional load B. The restart with load B: the initial state has internal forces from load A's equilibrium; DC's augmented system absorbs via lambda... The first step: residual = J^T(F_int - 0*B) = J^T F_int ≠ 0 (F_int balances A's load, not zero). Newton solves K dq - B_red dlambda =