{"id": "reasoning-a2f24188-7315-508c-9865-5b8ec7d89691-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792347039636, "updatedAt": 1792347039636, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now the second module: tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3_continuation.py — the EP direct/interp continuation. This is the big one. Let me design it carefully.\n\nModel: EP isolation geometry (from ep_isolation/ep_edge_coupling): L=1.0, W=0.24, t=0.012, shell NX=2, NY=1; stiffener 10x40mm at e=+0.04, fiber plasticity CS; S355 curve scale 1.0 (plastic) or 1e4 (elastic control). Direct: beam 2 elements (3 nodes at shell edge nodes); interp: beam 3 elements (4 nodes, middle ones interpolated).\n\nLoads: -2000 N/node on tip shell nodes (NY+1=2 nodes) for preyield and DC proportional load.\n\nDC: control = tip probe = grid[(NX,0)] shell node uz; target -0.20; num_steps 12, max_iterations 40, tolerance 1e-8. Split: half 6 steps to -0.10; resume 6 steps to -0.20 with initial_displacements/initial_element_states deep-copied, equilibrate_initial_state=False, emit_restart_checkpoint=False. Uninterrupted 12 steps.\n\nAssertions (per continuation item 2):\n- preyield: status completed, load_factor 1.0, plastic strain zero (alpha_max == 0), plastic == elastic control (uz diff tiny).\n- dc_full (direct & interp): status completed; |uz - (-0.20)| <= 1e-8; positive plastic strain (alpha_max > 0); independent relative residual at result.load_factor <= 1e-7; constraint postcheck passed (existing tolerances); full force/wrench reactions balance; input immutability of the model? (model is fresh per solve).\n- dc_split: half completed; resume completed; final u/lambda/history vs uninterrupted within restart tolerances; stitch histories; deep input immutability (supplied copies unchanged); reuse same inputs after → clean resume reproduces.\n- dc_cancel: progress_callback cancellation after two accepted increments → SolveCancelled; caller inputs unchanged; snapshots of the half result intact; reuse inputs → successful resume.\n- rejected-step snapshots: a DC run with max_iterations=1 (or 2) so a step fails → snapshots only from accepted steps; returned state = last accepted.\n\nStructure to control runtime: module-scoped fixtures computing expensive results once:\n- fixture `ep_geometry` (params via fixture params? pytest fixtures can't parametrize easily; use module-level cache keyed by coupling).\n\nLet me use a module-level cache dict + helper functions with lru_cache-like memo. Since tests run in one process, a simple dict cache works:\n\n```python\n_RESULTS = {}\ndef _cached(key, fn):\n    if key not in _RESULTS:\n        _RESULTS[key] = fn()\n    return _RESULTS[key]\n```\n\nTests:\n1. test_preyield_direct / test_preyield_interp (4 solves each? plastic+elastic = 2 solves per coupling) — cached.\n2. test_dc_full_direct/interp (1 solve each).\n3. test_dc_split_direct/interp (half + resume + uninterrupted; plus immutability checks).\n4. test_dc_cancel_direct (cancel + reuse resume).\n5. test_rejected_step_snapshots_exclude_rejected_history_direct (small failing run).\n\nRuntime estimate per coupling: preyield 2 solves (~6s), full 12-step (~30-60s), half (~20-30s), resume (~20-30s), uninterrupted (~30-60s), cancel (~10s), rejected (~5s). Total per coupling ~2-4 min. Both couplings ~5-8 min. That's 300-480s of numerical budget — significant but required. Under the harness: run the module in 2 attempts (direct tests via -k, interp via -k) to stay under 120s each? Each coupling's total might exceed 120s. Hmm.\n\nSplit the module run into multiple harness attempts with -k expressions:\n- attempt 1: preyield + dc_full (both couplings): ~2×(6+45) ≈ 100-150s. Risky.\n- Better: per-coupling attempts: -k \"direct\" (~2-4 min)... still >120s.\n\nHmm. The 120s diagnostic cap is a real constraint. Options:\n(a) Run each test function as its own harness attempt: pytest tests/...continuation.py::test_dc_full_direct etc. Each <=120s hopefully. That's ~10-12 attempts × overhead (~2s preflight each) — fine, budget is what matters.\n(b) The final regression runs the whole module in one 600s process — the module total ~5-8 min = 300-480s + historical modules... The final regression includes 25 historical modules + slice1/2 + slice3 modules. Historical 364 tests took how long historically? The baseline364 was \"historical\". The two standard-edge modules took 40s; slice12 67s. The 25 modules include heavier ones (test_nonlinear_restart_checkpoint, test_e4_pl_workflow_parity, test_general_contact_plane...). Could be 200-400s. Plus slice3 modules 300-480s. Total could exceed 600s!\n\nHmm. That's a problem for the final regression gate. Options: the portable runner balances modules over workers — with --workers N it splits modules across processes. Default workers? Let me check run_portable_ci.py defaults. If it uses multiple workers, wall time is less. But \"one effective numerical thread\" and \"One task numerical child at a time\" — the portable runner with workers>1 would spawn multiple pytest processes. The assignment says \"Use scripts/run_portable_ci.py --development <targets> --jit off --timeout-seconds 600 --memory-limit-mib 2048\" — no --workers specified, so default. Let me check the default workers.\n\nAlso I could reduce the continuation module's cost: the DC solves are the expensive part. Can't reduce steps (12 required). \n\nAlternatively, mark the expensive continuation tests to be skipped in the final regression? No — \"Final exact25 historical + two Slice1/2 + newSlice3 modules through portable runner\" — the new Slice3 modules must run. If the total exceeds 600s, the runner times out and the gate fails. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me reconsider the DC solve cost. The preyield stage (4 solves, 6 steps each, elastic+plastic at -2k) took 12.6s → ~3s per 6-step solve. The -6k plastic force solve took 53s BUT with 9 cutbacks (≈15 effective steps) and snapshot recording... ~3.5s per step. DC 12 steps at -0.20 target: how many Newton iterations per step? Maybe 3-6 → 12 steps × 5 iter × 0.35s/iter ≈ 21s? Hmm, the 53s case: 6 steps + 9 cutbacks = 15 steps... but cutback steps also iterate up to 30 times each. Total iterations maybe 100+ → 0.5s/iteration. DC 12 steps × ~5 iter = 60 iterations ≈ 30s. Half ≈ 15s, resume ≈ 15s. Per coupling: preyield 6s + full 30s + half 15s + resume 15s + uninterrupted 30s + cancel 8s + rejected 3s ≈ 107s. Both couplings ≈ 215s. Plus slice3 repairs module: R1 tests cheap (~2s), R2 cheap, R3 cheap (2 solves tiny), R4: 2 parametrized solves (6 steps each ~2-4s) + dispatch-jump (2 solves) + derivative FD test (n_red ~ 30 columns × 2 assemblies each = 60+2 assemblies ≈ 20s?) + R5 (pre-solve 6 steps + DC 2 steps ≈ 5s). Say ~60-90s. Slice1/2: 67s. Historical 25 modules: unknown, maybe 150-400s.\n\nTotal: 215 + 90 + 67 + historical. If historical is 300s → ~670s > 600s cap. Tight. The portable runner may use multiple workers though. Let me check its default.", "summary": []}
