{"id": "reasoning-84281042-b4f6-5f31-839c-aea43d02eb15-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792347062804, "updatedAt": 1792347062804, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Excellent data: the historical 22-module regression (343 passed) took 141.89s in one process with JIT off on a worktree machine. The 25-module set (22 + 3 supplements) — the receipt shows 22 targets (the selection.json lists 22). Wait: \"Final exact 25 historical modules = targets in selection.json PLUS tests/test_instance_dict_descriptor_layout.py, test_general_contact_native_deadline_scale.py, test_general_contact_native_replay_diagnostics.py\" — 22+3=25. The historical run covered the 22 in 142s. The 3 supplements are contact/deadline tests — maybe 30-60s more. So historical ≈ 180-220s.\n\nBudget for final regression: ~200s (historical) + 67s (slice12) + slice3 repairs (~60-90s) + continuation (~215s) ≈ 545-590s. Just under 600s! Tight but feasible. And the harness preflight adds ~2s.\n\nTo be safer, I can trim the continuation module cost:\n- Cache solves across tests (planned).\n- The rejected-step test: use few steps (e.g., num_steps=3, max_iterations=1) — cheap.\n- The cancel test: cancel after 2 accepted steps of a 6-step resume — ~2 steps ≈ 5-8s.\n\nOK. Now let me also confirm: the portable runner --development runs pytest with the given files in one process; the module cache works within that process. But when I run the continuation module in pieces via -k under the harness (during evidence collection), each pytest process recomputes the cached solves for the selected tests. E.g., running test_dc_split_direct alone computes half+resume+uninterrupted (~60s). Running test_dc_cancel_direct alone needs the cancel run only (~8s) IF it doesn't depend on the split fixtures. Design the cache so each test only computes what it needs.\n\nPlan for evidence runs (each ≤120s):\n1. pytest tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py (repairs module) — one attempt (~60-90s?).\n2. pytest continuation -k \"preyield or dc_full\" (both couplings: 4 preyield solves ~12s + 2 full ~60s) ≈ 75s.\n3. pytest continuation -k \"direct and (split or cancel or rejected)\" (~half 15 + resume 15 + uninterp 30 + cancel 8 + rejected 3 ≈ 71s).\n4. pytest continuation -k \"interp and (split or cancel)\" (similar ~68s).\n\nTotal continuation evidence ≈ 215s + repairs 90s ≈ 305s. Plus mesh study + arc reference. Budget: 2078s non-reserve. 305 + mesh (300-600) + arc (300-900) ≈ 900-1800. OK, feasible but I must be careful.\n\nNow — write the continuation module. Key design:\n\n```python\nL, W, T = 1.0, 0.24, 0.012\nNX, NY = 2, 1\nR = (0,0,0.04)\nCS = fiber plasticity dict\nDC_PARAMS = num_steps 12, max_iterations 40, tolerance 1e-8\nFC_PARAMS = num_steps 6, max_iterations 30\n\ndef _material(model, scale): S355 curve\ndef _build(coupling, scale): model, load, probe, grid  (direct: 2 beam elems; interp: 3)\ndef _add_tip_load(m, lc, fz=-2000)\ndef _alpha_max(states)\ndef _independent_residual(model, load, u, states, lam) -> relative residual at lam\ndef _wrench_balance(model, result, load) -> force/moment imbalance\n_CACHE = {}\n\nSolves (cached by key):\n- preyield: (coupling, 'plastic'|'elastic')\n- dc_full: (coupling,)\n- dc_half: (coupling,)\n- dc_resume: (coupling,) — resume from deep copies\n- dc_uninterrupted: (coupling,)\n- dc_cancel: (coupling,) — cancelled run info\n- dc_rejected: (coupling,) — failing run\n```\n\nImmutability checks: for the split test, before resume: deep copies of half.displacements and half.element_states; after resume: assert unchanged (compare with _deep_equal). The resume solve itself uses the copies as initial_displacements/initial_element_states.\n\nFor \"deliberately rejected resume\": resume with max_iterations=1 (fails first step) → status stopped_at_limit/diverged; inputs unchanged. Then \"reuse the same untouched input for a clean successful resume\" — the cached clean resume uses the same input objects (must be the same deep copies!). So the cache must store the input copies and the clean resume must be run AFTER the rejected resume to prove reuse... but caching order: if the clean resume is computed first (for the split comparison), the \"reuse after rejected\" claim needs another clean resume run (~15s). Hmm. The addendum: \"Prove unchanged after a successful resume, a deliberately rejected resume and token cancellation; then reuse the same untouched input for a clean successful resume.\" So sequence: clean resume (for comparison) → rejected resume → cancellation → final clean resume (reuse). That's 2 clean resumes + 1 rejected + 1 cancelled per coupling. Extra ~15s per coupling. Acceptable: per coupling ≈ 122s. Hmm, that pushes the -k attempts over 120s. Reorganize evidence runs:\n- attempt: \"-k direct and split\" (half+resume+uninterrupted+rejected ≈ 15+15+30+3 = 63s)\n- attempt: \"-k direct and cancel\" (cancel run + reuse-resume ≈ 8+15 = 23s)\n- same for interp.\n\nFine.\n\nNow the cancellation mechanics: progress_callback — signature? emit_progress(progress_callback, event_type, stage, **fields). The callback receives... let me check emit_progress/ProgressCallback signature to write the callback correctly.", "summary": []}
