{"id": "reasoning-bfe31e6f-9940-5089-a6ba-c0f251ce53e6-0", "sessionId": "d77c8371-229c-7034-6088-eaefcc4b4445", "turnId": "turn-6f19a1036e2fefd5642f253cb635b74b", "createdAt": 1792347084448, "updatedAt": 1792347084448, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Good. The progress callback receives a ProgressEvent (mapping). The DC emit_progress passes event_type=\"nonlinear_static_step\", stage=\"nonlinear_static.displacement\", completed=local_step_index, plus metadata keys (control, step_index, load_factor, ...). So the callback:\n\n```python\ndef on_progress(event):\n    if event.event_type == \"nonlinear_static_step\" and event.stage == \"nonlinear_static.displacement\":\n        accepted += 1\n        if accepted >= 2:\n            token.cancel(\"...\")\n```\n\nNow write the continuation module. Also need the wrench balance helper. And the independent residual at actual load_factor.\n\nFor the reactions: result.steps[-1].support_reactions[\"root\"] → tuple of 6 (summed covectors). Force balance: root[:3] + lam*sum(applied) ≈ 0. Wait — sign convention: support_reactions = _support_reaction_resultants(model, reaction_imbalance) where reaction_imbalance = F_int - F_ext transferred. At the root dofs, F_int - F_ext = -(reaction force applied by support)? The existing test asserted: root_reaction + applied_total_force ≈ 0. So root_reaction[:3] ≈ -applied. Good, same convention.\n\nMoment balance with deformed arms: total moment about origin of (reactions + applied) ≈ 0. Reactions: at root nodes r_i (current pos = ref + u), force f_i = the per-node reaction... but support_reactions only gives the SUM over the BC's dofs, not per-node. Hmm! The root BC has 2 nodes (ny=1 → 2 root nodes); the resultant tuple sums both nodes' forces and both nodes' moment covectors. To compute the reaction moment about the origin with deformed arms, I need per-node forces. \n\nAlternative: compute the wrench balance independently from the full imbalance vector: I have F_int (from _assemble_nonlinear_system at the returned state) and F_ext = lam*F_prop. The full residual R = F_int - F_ext. At the free dofs R≈0 (equilibrium); at constrained dofs R = reaction. So per-node reaction at root node i: r_node = R[dofs] (6 components: force + rotation covector). Then:\n- Force balance: sum over root nodes R[trans] + lam*sum(applied forces) ≈ 0 — automatically true since F_int is self-equilibrated? Actually F_int from a converged state has zero resultant... not exactly; the balance: sum_all R = 0 (internal forces self-equilibrium for the whole body + applied). Let me do it cleanly:\n  total_force = sum over ALL dofs of (F_int - F_ext) restricted to translations per node... The global equilibrium: sum of all nodal internal forces = 0 (for stress in equilibrium, plus the applied external). Actually the residual at free dofs ≈ 0, at root dofs = reactions. Global force balance: reactions + applied = 0 → check: sum over root nodes of R[0:3] + lam * F_prop_translations_sum ≈ 0.\n- Moment about origin: sum over root nodes [r_i_current × f_i + m_cov_i] + sum over loaded nodes [r_j_current × (lam*F_j)] ≈ 0. Where m_cov is the rotation covector at the node; for the root nodes theta=0 → covector = spatial moment. For loaded tip nodes, applied loads are pure forces (no applied moments) → moment = r_j × F_j.\n\nThis is a genuine independent wrench check using deformed arms. The residual R at root dofs: I need F_int at the returned state — I already compute it for the independent residual. \n\nBut careful: the transferred imbalance — the beam slaves' reactions get transferred to masters; the root BC constrains only shell nodes; the slave beam nodes are MPC slaves (their dofs are dependent, not free). The residual at slave dofs is nonzero (F_int - F_ext at slave dofs) but is carried by the coupling. For the wrench balance, use the TRANSFERRED imbalance (transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters) so slave forces are moved to master shell nodes; then sum over ALL nodes: total force = sum over all nodes of transferred R[trans]... For global equilibrium the transferred residual should have zero total force and zero total moment about any point (since it equals F_int - F_ext distributed, and F_int has zero resultant wrench for a self-equilibrated stress field... hmm, F_int resultant is zero only if the internal stress is in equilibrium without body forces — yes for discrete FE internal forces: sum of all nodal internal forces = 0 and moment = 0 (for a consistent formulation). Then transferred R keeps the same total wrench (transfer preserves total force and total moment? transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters moves f_b to masters with weights and adds moment_covector = m_b + A.T f_b — the total force is preserved (sum w = 1); the total moment: the covector addition accounts for the lever arm... For the wrench identity, the transfer preserves the total spatial wrench (that's the design). So:\n\ntotal_wrench(R_transferred) = wrench(F_int) - wrench(F_ext) = 0 - wrench(F_ext) → wrench(R_transferred) + wrench(F_ext) = 0.\n\nSo the check: compute R = F_int - lam*F_prop (full vectors); transfer via map; then:\n- sum over all nodes: force_sum = sum R_trans[trans dofs per node] ≈ -lam*sum(F_prop trans) → assert force_sum + lam*F_prop_sum ≈ 0.\n- moment about origin: sum over nodes [r_i × f_i + m_i] + lam * sum_j [r_j × F_j] ≈ 0, where for the second term only loaded nodes contribute (F_prop is zero elsewhere).\n\nSince R_transferred is nonzero only at constrained (root) dofs (free dofs ≈ 0, slave dofs zeroed by transfer), the sums are effectively over root nodes + masters. I'll compute over all nodes for correctness.\n\nFor rotation covectors at nodes with theta≠0 (the masters have finite rotations!), covector ≠ spatial moment. The conversion: m_spatial = J_l(theta)^{-T} p? From the reference review: \"p = J_l(theta)^T m, m = J_l(theta)^{-T} p\" — wait that gives m from p: m = J_l^{-T} p. Hmm: delta_psi = J_l delta_theta; virtual work: m·delta_theta = p·delta_psi = p·J_l delta_theta → m = J_l^T p → p = J_l^{-T} m. So m = J_l^T p. And corotational.py has spatial_moment_from_rotation_covector(theta, p) — the existing test uses it: `spatial_moment_from_rotation_covector(theta_bar, m_b + A.T @ f_b)` returns the spatial torque. So use that function per node.\n\nOK — this is implementable. The wrench check:\n\n```python\ndef _wrench_imbalance(model, u, f_int, f_ext, mpc_map):\n    R = f_int - f_ext\n    R_t = mpc_map.transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters(u, R) if mpc_map else R\n    force = np.zeros(3); moment = np.zeros(3)\n    for node in model.mesh.nodes.values():\n        dofs = model.mesh.dof_manager.get_node_dofs(node.id)\n        f = R_t[dofs[:3]]; p = R_t[dofs[3:6]]\n        r = np.array([node.x, node.y, node.z]) + u[dofs[:3]]\n        force += f\n        theta = u[dofs[3:6]]\n        moment += np.cross(r, f) + spatial_moment_from_rotation_covector(theta, p)\n    ext_force = ... sum over nodes of f_ext translations\n    ext_moment = sum over nodes r_j × f_ext_j (applied forces; no applied moments in these cases)\n    return force + ext_force, moment + ext_moment\n```\n\nAssert norms small relative to reference (|lam*F_total| * L).\n\nHmm wait — is the internal force wrench exactly zero? For corotational FE with consistent internal forces, sum of nodal forces = 0 and sum of moments = 0 holds for the assembled internal force vector (self-equilibrium of internal stress resultants) — up to discretization... Actually no! The assembled internal nodal forces satisfy: sum over nodes f_int = 0 only if the element internal force vectors are self-equilibrated (each element's nodal forces sum to zero and have zero moment). For corotational elements this holds (equilibrium of the element). For the shell Q4 with plasticity — should hold too (internal force derived from stress integration; rigid-body translation/rotation invariance implies zero resultant wrench). Yes, standard.\n\nBut careful: the moment balance uses the CURRENT positions r_i = ref + u. The internal force wrench about the origin: sum r_i × f_i,int + m_i,int(cov→spatial) = 0 for internal forces (objectivity). OK.\n\nSo the wrench check is a strong independent equilibrium assertion. \n\nNow, the independent residual: reconstruct at result.load_factor:\n```python\ndef _independent_relative_residual(model, load, u, states, lam):\n    K0,_ = assemble_stiffness_matrix(model)\n    F_prop,_ = assemble_load_vector(model, load)\n    _,_,T,u0,_,_ = build_constraint_transformation(K0, F_prop, model)\n    mpc_map = build_nonlinear_rotating_mpc_map(model, T)\n    states_copy = deepcopy(states)\n    F_int,_,_ = _assemble_nonlinear_system(model, u, states_copy, 5, tangent=False, kinematics=\"corotational\", corotational_tangent=\"work_conjugate\")\n    resid = mpc_map.reduce_force(u, lam*F_prop - F_int)\n    ref = mpc_map.reduce_force(u, max(abs(lam),1.0)*F_prop)\n    return norm(resid)/max(norm(ref),1.0)\n```\nAssert <= 1e-7.\n\nConstraint postcheck: result.info[\"constraint_postcheck\"][\"status\"] == \"passed\" and max residuals <= existing tolerances (the Slice1/2 test used <= 1e-12).\n\nPlastic strain positive: alpha_max > 0 and steps[-1].max_equivalent_plastic_strain > 0.\n\nSplit comparison tolerances: I'll compute and use:\n- displacements: np.testing.assert_allclose(resumed, uninterrupted, rtol=1e-8, atol=1e-10) — the existing restart tolerances. If this fails at runtime I'll reassess with evidence.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me think again about whether resumed vs uninterrupted will agree to 1e-10. Key insight from before: half steps 1-6 and uninterrupted steps 1-6 have identical targets but slightly different constraint tolerance (1e-9 vs 2e-9 scale). Newton iterates: both start from q=0, lam=0. Same targets, same convergence criterion except constraint_error threshold (1e-8*0.1=1e-9 vs 1e-8*0.2=2e-9). The iterate sequence is IDENTICAL until the stopping test differs. Stopping: the first iterate meeting BOTH criteria. With 1e-9 vs 2e-9 thresholds, the accepted iterate could differ only if some iterate has constraint_error in (1e-9, 2e-9] and residual ok — then half accepts it, uninterrupted does one more iteration. Newton's final iterates: typically constraint error drops from ~1e-6 to ~1e-13 in one iteration (quadratic). The probability of landing in (1e-9,2e-9] is low but nonzero. So half step-6 state ≈ uninterrupted step-6 state, possibly differing by one Newton iteration's refinement (~1e-9-ish state diff).\n\nThen resume steps 1-6 (local) start from the half state with lam=0 guess; uninterrupted steps 7-12 start from step-6 state with lam=lam6. Both converge to the same per-step equilibria (same targets, up to the ~1e-9 initial offset). The per-step accepted states differ by ~the accumulated offset ~1e-9-1e-8.\n\nSo displacements diff ~1e-9..1e-8: rtol 1e-8 (uz=0.2 → 2e-9 tolerance) might fail marginally; atol 1e-10 definitely too small. I'll write the test to assert with the existing tolerances BUT... risk of failure. \n\nDecision: I'll first write a diagnostic assertion with generous capture: compute the diffs and assert against the existing tolerances; if the run fails, the failure output gives the actual diff, and I adjust with documented justification. But each continuation run costs ~60s... Alternatively assert with tolerances that are defensible a priori: the comparison tolerance cannot be tighter than the solver's own convergence tolerance (1e-8 relative residual → state uncertainty ~1e-9..1e-8 m). The \"existing restart tolerances\" (1e-8/1e-10) were used with tolerance=1e-10 solves. Scaling: with solve tolerance 1e-8 (100x looser), the equivalent comparison tolerance is ~100x looser: rtol 1e-6, atol 1e-8. I'll use rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-8 for displacements, rtol=1e-6 for load factor, alpha rtol=1e-5/atol=1e-9, and document: \"existing restart comparison tolerances scaled by the DC convergence tolerance ratio (1e-8/1e-10)\". That's principl