ing_mpc_map.apply_kinematics(\n                        fully_constrained_displacement\n                    ),\n                    dtype=float,\n                ).reshape(-1)\n            fully_constrained_displacement = fully_constrained_displacement.copy()\n```\n\nWait — for force_restart, initial_committed_displacement = map(projected_free + offset). When n_red==0, projected_free = T@q... hmm, is T@q exactly zero when n_red==0? T has shape (n_full, n_red) = (n_full, 0), so T@q is zeros(n_full). Yes. So map(projected_free + offset) = map(offset). Good — already mapped exactly once.\n\nFor checkpoint_resume: initial_committed_displacement is the mapped reconstruction, verified array_equal against supplied_full. Good.\n\nFor fresh: map(u0). For fresh force, u0 is the prescribed proportional field — the reviewer says fresh must use map(u0). Note: for the fresh non-force branch (displacement control), initial_committed_displacement = map(T@q + u0) = map(u0) when n_red==0 — identical. So using map(u0) uniformly for fresh is consistent.\n\nHmm, one subtlety: for fresh force, is u0 guaranteed to be the full prescribed vector? u0 is the affine offset from prescribed supports. Yes.\n\nBut wait — there's also the state preparation issue: `_prepare_qualified_q4_states_for_nonlinear_solve` and `_activate_nonlinear_state_storage` were called with initial_committed_displacement BEFORE this branch. For fresh force, that's map(T@q)=0, not map(u0). In the original code, they were called with the raw initial_committed_displacement (unmapped T@q+u0 or T@q). So the original also passed the \"wrong\" (linear/zero) vector to state preparation for the mapped route. The reviewer's bounded repair only concerns the fully constrained return vector: \"Construct the full constrained vector through the map before all evaluation/recovery/return uses\" — hmm, \"before all evaluation/recovery/return uses\". The constitutive evaluation inside the branch uses fully_constrained_displacement (fixed).